\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Makes New Offer to Union\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 312 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  on Monday made a new offer to its striking machinists union in hopes of ending a walkout that is costing the cash-strapped jet maker hundreds of millions of dollars a week.\par \par \uc2 The proposal comes 11 days after \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's biggest union overwhelmingly voted to go on strike after rejecting a deal struck between \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and union leaders.\par \par \uc2 Union officials Monday said they had no input into the new offer and criticized the company for sharing it with union members and the news media.\par \par \uc2 "This proposal does not go far enough to address your concerns, and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has missed the mark with this proposal," the union said in a note to members. "They are trying to drive a wedge between our members and weaken our solidarity with this divisive strategy."\par \par \uc2 The latest proposal calls for a 30% pay increase over four years, up from the 25% in the previous deal.\par \par \uc2 It also doubles the ratification bonus to $6,000, restores annual bonuses the company initially proposed eliminating and increases company contributions to employee 401(k) plans.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said workers have until Friday to ratify the deal. The company said it would keep its commitment to build its next new airplane in the unionized Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 The union expressed frustration with the company and said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  wasn't addressing members' top concerns.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , meanwhile, rolled out a series of cost-cutting moves including a hiring freeze, temporary furlough and executive pay cuts for the duration of the strike. The jet maker had been burning through roughly $1 billion a month before the strike and credit-ratings firms warned that a prolonged work stoppage would lead them to downgrade \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to junk status.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's 33,000-member machinists union walked out Sept. 13 after rejecting a labor deal recommended by its leaders.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240924ek9o0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
