\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Bosses Rejoice! \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Delivers the End of Hybrid Work --- If you thought your two days a week of work-from-home were safe, think again. The CEO of one of America's largest employers just called everyone back to the office full time.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vanessa Fuhrmans, Katherine Bindley and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,779 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  set CEOs abuzz with envy -- and white-collar workers with fear -- this past week with a surprise memo calling corporate staffers back to the office full time.\par \par \uc2 Now, long after hybrid work seemed a settled matter at many companies, suddenly both sides are wondering: Who's next?\par \par \uc2 At a party in Seattle Tuesday evening, shortly after Jassy went public with his plan, his return-to-office rally cry was a hot topic among executives in attendance.\par \par \uc2 "It was the talk of the town," says \uc2 Glenn Kelman\uc2 , CEO of Seattle-based real-estate brokerage \uc2 Redfin\uc2 , who was there.\par \par \uc2 Until Jassy's memo, 4 1/2 years after the Covid-19 pandemic sent everyone home, bosses and employees had largely reached a truce on part-time remote work. Many company leaders looked out at their substantially empty offices in quiet exasperation. But they feared that forcing their employees to come to the office more often could send top performers fleeing for more flexible work setups elsewhere. The handful of companies that have returned to full-time, in-person work, including \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , have been outliers. The number of firms requiring five days in the office has actually fallen by 15% from a year ago, according to data from Flex Index, which tracks the work policies of more than 6,300 companies.\par \par \uc2 But a tougher labor market, especially for white-collar professionals, is now changing the calculus. With jobs harder to find and more companies willing to cut them, the balance of power is shifting from workers to bosses. Many of those bosses still worry that productivity and innovation suffer when people aren't together in an office. With Jassy laying down the law at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , some executives predict more full-time office mandates will now follow.\par \par \uc2 In a \uc2 KPMG\uc2  survey of 400 U.S. CEOs released this week, nearly 80% said that they expected corporate employees to be in offices full time within the next three years. That's more than double the 34% who said so in April.\par \par \uc2 Kelman said other CEOs will be watching \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for two things: Will \uc2 Amazon\uc2  bleed workers? Or will this give it a competitive edge?\par \par \uc2 "There's one world in which \uc2 Amazon\uc2  loses talent -- it doesn't become an employer of choice," says Kelman. "And there's another world where \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is able to innovate faster, is able to resolve snafus more quickly."\par \par \uc2 Redfin\uc2  employees -- currently expected to be in the office two days a week -- have already queried Kelman about whether he'll follow suit, he says. Though he has no plans to require more days, he says, hybrid work is harder than everyone thought it would be.\par \par \uc2 "It's working," he says. "But it's hard just as a physical fact to pay for an office that is mostly empty."\par \par \uc2 In his note to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers announcing the change, Jassy said that the new policy will help both the company and its employees.\par \par \uc2 "We've observed that it's easier for our teammates to learn, model, practice, and strengthen our culture," he wrote about office work. "[C]ollaborating, brainstorming, and inventing are simpler and more effective; teaching and learning from one another are more seamless; and, teams tend to be better connected to one another."\par \par \uc2 Some current and former \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees suspect that Jassy isn't just interested in more collaboration and connection. They blasted Jassy's memo as being tantamount to a layoff announcement for workers who will be alienated by the new policy.\par \par \uc2 "The fact he didn't use the word 'layoffs' doesn't change the meaning of the lengthy email he sent to company employees explaining a fresh round of flagrantly unpopular and alienating policy changes," wrote Tony Carr, a former \uc2 Amazon\uc2  general manager who left the company late last year, on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesman said any inferences about motive beyond what Jassy laid out in his memo are inaccurate. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  doesn't plan to reduce overall headcount as part of its new policy, he added.\par \par \uc2 Jassy said in his memo that the company understands that some workers will need to make adjustments to their personal lives to accommodate working in the office five days a week, which was why the new policy wouldn't go into effect until Jan. 2.\par \par \uc2 Other return-to-office orders have sparked worker exoduses. Nearly half the staffers at \uc2 Grindr\uc2  resigned last fall after the dating app shifted from a "remote-first" policy to requiring office attendance twice a week, according to the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2 . Some \uc2 Farmers Group\uc2  employees quit last year after the insurer said the majority of Farmers employees should be in the office three days a week. A few months later, Farmers cut 2,400 jobs, or 11% of its workforce.\par \par \uc2 The problem for bosses, though, is that it's often high-performing employees who leave, since they have the best odds of getting hired elsewhere, says \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  economist \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 . "Managers are very happy to tell underperformers, 'You gotta come in or you're out of here," he adds. With more coveted employees, "they often just don't want to enforce it, because it impacts their own bonus from promotions."\par \par \uc2 CJ Felli, a 29-year-old systems-development engineer with \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , added an "Open to Work" banner to his \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profile not long after \uc2 Amazon\uc2  made its announcement. He's hunting for a new job and says the company's new policy was a tipping point.\par \par \uc2 Felli lives only 15 minutes from his office in Seattle and doesn't mind going in three days a week as the company has been requiring since last year; lately he's been going in almost every day. He's a huge fan of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's culture and says getting a job there was the proudest moment of his life. But he fears as a result of the new policy that the company will lose a lot of its midlevel talent, especially parents and those who have long commutes.\par \par \uc2 "We are not going to be able to flourish and survive long term if we're just an entry-level college shop," he says.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to overstate how much remote and hybrid work have reshaped the postpandemic labor market. It has enabled moves to lower-cost areas, let working parents better coordinate child care and brought millions of people into the workforce -- including those with disabilities. And it made it easier for mothers of young children to stay on the job, helping drive a sharp increase in the number of women working.\par \par \uc2 Tech-industry workers especially took advantage of the ability to work remotely, flocking from high-cost coast cities to cheaper locales such as Salt Lake City, Utah, and Boise, Idaho. High in demand, many commanded the same pay they made in San Francisco and Seattle. "Work from anywhere" became a favorite recruiting tactic, with some workers being told they'd never need to come back to the office.\par \par \uc2 Remote work also fueled a digital commerce boom that let online retail giants like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  reap record profits, and hire hundreds of thousands of people, many in far-flung places. Over 2020 and 2021, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's head count roughly doubled to more than 1.6 million employees. Then the company laid off 27,000 workers starting in late 2022.\par \par \uc2 The once red-hot demand for tech talent has been cooling as the industry adjusts its labor needs and shifts resources into artificial intelligence. Postings for software development jobs are down more than 30% since February 2020, according to \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2 . And industry layoffs that began in late 2022 have continued this year: Tech companies have shed around 137,000 jobs since January, according to Layoffs.fyi.\par \par \uc2 Returning to the office five days a week may prove too difficult for many companies. All of that remote pandemic hiring means many companies' workforces are far more scattered than before. Nearly a third of workers at large firms last year didn't work in the same metro area as their managers, up from about 23% in February 2020, according to data from payroll provider ADP.\par \par \uc2 "For us, and for many CEOs at this time, bringing everyone back fully would be so disruptive -- not just to the company, but to employees' lives as well," said David Ko, CEO of Calm, a mental-health app. Calm shifted to remote work at the pandemic's onset in 2020. Nearby staff now typically come into one of its six office hubs anywhere from one to five days a week, depending on the role, and the company periodically brings some teams together for two- to three-day collaboration sprints on specific projects.\par \par \uc2 Will companies succeed in coaxing remote workers back into offices? The answer likely hinges on hiring demand. Economists \uc2 David Autor\uc2 , Arindrajit Dube and Annie McGrew have found that the share of people working from home was significantly higher in states with tight labor markets during the 2021 to 2023 period than states with looser markets.\par \par \uc2 For now, bosses are likely to get more questions from their workers wondering if they need to get ready to be in the office more often.\par \par \uc2 In a meeting with \uc2 Intuit\uc2 's New York office this past week, employees pressed CEO \uc2 Sasan Goodarzi\uc2  to address \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's move, and to clarify whether the company would change its own policy. The maker of TurboTax software generally asks employees to show up in person at least two days a week.\par \par \uc2 Goodarzi told them he'd like them to come to the office a bit more -- say, three days a week -- but didn't call for a full in-office return. He has told employees before that he believes the current ways of working could still evolve, based on what \uc2 Intuit\uc2  needs.\par \par \uc2 In an interview afterward, he said that employee surveys and badge-tracking data show \uc2 Intuit\uc2 's most engaged staffers typically come in three to four days. Those who are there one day, or less, tend to be weaker performers.\par \par \uc2 "There's a massive experiment going on," said Goodarzi of corporate work arrangements. "I think it's important that we remain curious as to what's the optimal answer."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Justin Lahart contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Workers of the World, \uc2 Unite\uc2 !" -- WSJ Sept. 30, 2024)\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich396\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal5940
