\par\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's Office Return Sparks Staff Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera and Joseph De Avila \par \uc2 375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 's decision to require its corporate staff members to be in the office every weekday sparked frustration among employees and curiosity among executives at other companies interested in seeing worker reaction.\par \par \uc2 Some at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said they were surprised and disappointed. On social-media sites, employees complained about added commute times. Others were fine with the change, saying they were already in the office at least four days a week.\par \par \uc2 "People are not happy about it," said one \uc2 Amazon\uc2  software engineer in an interview on Tuesday. "It seems unreasonable and stands against data that people are still productive out of the office."\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's new office policy marks a significant shift for a tech industry where most employers have embraced hybrid work. Only 7% of large tech companies require employees to be in the office five days a week, compared with 33% for all U.S. companies, according to Flex Index, a software firm that tracks return-to-office efforts. Tech companies have often spearheaded major shifts in office policies during and after the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the new return-to-office norms, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said Monday that it might be thinning out its management staff. In an internal memo to employees, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  left open the possibility that some positions might be eliminated as it restructured its departments to have fewer managers per team.\par \par \uc2 "It's possible that organizations may identify roles that are no longer required," the internal memo said.\par \par \uc2 Critics fear the moves may allow \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to shrink its workforce without official layoffs. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said its return-to-office rules are separate from its restructuring plans. A company spokesman said the changes are an effort to strengthen its culture.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives defended the changes by pointing to the hundreds of thousands of company warehouse employees who have had to go in to work each day, even during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "I think about all of our fulfillment center workers, they show up in the office every day," Dharmesh Mehta, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's vice president in its marketplace business, said Tuesday. "There's no way for them to get their jobs done without being right there in the fulfillment center."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240918ek9i0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
