\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2 's Fight With \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Over Jobs Escalates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  is once again taking aim at the Jeep maker, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , after threatening to go on strike at the company's U.S. factories over delays in reopening an idled plant in Illinois and other points of contention.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain, talking to members Tuesday evening, said the union was taking the next steps to prepare for a work stoppage, including a completion of the grievance process and having some \uc2 UAW\uc2  locals take strike-authorization votes. While those votes don't lead to immediate action, they indicate members' support for a walkout.\par \par \uc2 "We're preparing to take actions on \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to enforce our contract and make this company keep the promise they made to the American people," Fain said in a live video address.\par \par \uc2 Tensions with the company continue to escalate less than a year after the signing of a new labor deal that ended a six-week strike.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the union has accused \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  of trying to shift some SUV production outside the U.S. and criticized Chief Executive \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2  over reductions in factory jobs. It also alleges that the company has failed to live up to commitments made in the 2023 contract agreement.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which is confronting restless dealers and a pileup of unsold cars in the U.S., has denied violating its labor contract with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and said the union would have no basis to strike.\par \par \uc2 The clash comes as automakers are adjusting to a changing U.S. car market that is now starting to come down from postpandemic highs. The availability of new-car stock is rising, putting downward pressure on pricing and leading car companies to spend more on profit-damaging discounts.\par \par \uc2 In the last round of contract talks, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  won a major victory. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agreed to reopen its assembly plant in Belvidere, Ill., which used to build the Jeep Cherokee and went dark in early 2023. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has committed to building a midsize truck at the factory and establishing a vehicle-parts hub there, although those plans have since been delayed.\par \par \uc2 Fain, the union's outspoken leader, in late August threatened to have members walk off the job over delays at the Illinois plant, saying \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  is backpedaling on its investment commitments.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, he said there is a multistep process under the current contract that the union has to complete before it can legally strike any plants. The last step would be meeting with the company several times to try to come up with a resolution.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which also owns Ram, Dodge and Chrysler, has argued that it has the right to adjust its factory plans, pointing to language in the union contract that gives it flexibility to make changes based on "market conditions" and other factors.\par \par \uc2 The car company is coming off a highly profitable period in North America, where a lack of inventory helped keep buyers paying high prices for new vehicles.\par \par \uc2 As interest rates climbed and the new-car shortage has eased, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's profit margin has slipped in the region. Within the past year, it has taken steps to trim its workforce, including the disclosure of plans to cut up to 2,450 \uc2 UAW\uc2 -represented workers at a truck plant in Warren, Mich., last month.\par \par \uc2 The automaker's share of the U.S. market fell to 8.5% in the first six months of the year from 10.4% a year earlier, according to \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 . In July, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said it would start making price adjustments and pull back on factory production by about 100,000 vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Dealers have urged \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to spend more money on financing deals and rebates to clear out bloated inventory. In a recent open letter, members of the company's dealer advisory council blamed Tavares for the company's problems in North America.\par \par \uc2 Fain, a former electrician at Chrysler who was elected president in 2023, has also taken aim at Tavares, criticizing the executive's nearly $40 million pay package last year.\par \par \uc2 "The problem isn't the little guy at the bottom; it's the big man at the top," Fain said in a video released in August.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, several \uc2 UAW\uc2  locals filed grievances against \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , saying \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  had plans to move factory work on the Dodge Durango outside the U.S.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ben Glickman contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240918ek9i0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
