\par\par \b \uc2 Cigna\uc2  Sues \uc2 FTC\uc2  Over Criticism of Drug Costs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Wilde Mathews \par \uc2 544 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One of the country's biggest pharmacy-benefit managers, Cigna Group's Express Scripts, sued the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 , demanding a retraction of a recent report critical of the companies that negotiate drug spending.\par \par \uc2 In the lawsuit, \uc2 Cigna\uc2 's \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2  pharmacy-benefit unit calls the agency's July report "seventy-four pages of unsupported innuendo" and takes aim at \uc2 FTC\uc2  Chair Lina Khan, arguing that she is biased against the industry.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, which the company filed Tuesday in federal court in St. Louis, heightens the conflict between the \uc2 FTC\uc2  and leading pharmacy-benefit managers, or PBMs, which play a critical role in paying for drugs.\par \par \uc2 It comes after The Wall Street Journal reported the \uc2 FTC\uc2  is preparing to sue the largest PBMs over their tactics for negotiating prices for drugs including insulin.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  said it stands by its study. "Just three companies control nearly 80% of the market that millions of Americans must use to purchase necessary drugs at high costs," an agency spokesman said. "This is a complicated and opaque market, and the \uc2 FTC\uc2  is committed to using its clear authority to help the public and policymakers understand it."\par \par \uc2 The companies are supposed to bring down the cost of medicines by negotiating with drug manufacturers and pharmacies, but the \uc2 FTC\uc2  said in its report that they sometimes steer patients away from less-expensive medicines and overcharge for cancer therapies.\par \par \uc2 Health insurers and employers hire PBMs to control spending on medicines. But some patients, pharmacists and lawmakers have attacked the companies, saying they profit from the complexity of the drug business.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  issued its report after a two-year investigation of PBM business practices, though it was framed as interim findings from the probe. States have also been examining the companies.\par \par \uc2 PBMs say they pass along to health plans and employers the discounts and other savings they negotiate and as a result, hold down spending. The firms blame pharmaceutical companies for high drug prices. They called the \uc2 FTC\uc2  report flawed.\par \par \uc2 In the lawsuit, \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2  said the \uc2 FTC\uc2  violated the company's constitutional right to due process and exceeded its statutory authority in releasing a report that included false and unsupported conclusions based on a biased process.\par \par \uc2 The company also said it had been defamed, claiming that the \uc2 FTC\uc2  largely ignored extensive documents that \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2  produced and instead relied "overwhelmingly on information cherry-picked from public sources and anonymous comments to fit the Commission's predetermined narrative that PBMs drive up drug prices and disadvantage independent pharmacies."\par \par \uc2 Andrea Nelson, chief legal officer of \uc2 Cigna Group\uc2 , said the goal of the suit was to have the report retracted. The complaint asks the court to issue an order "vacating and setting aside the report" and require the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to remove it from the agency's websites.\par \par \uc2 Companies targeted by the \uc2 FTC\uc2  regularly mount legal attacks on the agency, and Khan has been a particular focus.\par \par \uc2 The three largest pharmacy-benefit managers are part of healthcare conglomerates that include insurers and other companies. \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2 's \uc2 OptumRx\uc2  and \uc2 CVS Health\uc2 's Caremark, in addition to \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2 , which is part of \uc2 Cigna\uc2 's Evernorth arm, manage about 80% of U.S. prescriptions.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240918ek9i0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
