\par\par \b \uc2 Tiny Plastic Employees Are Lego Hit --- Collectors bug workers for their business cards\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kim Mackrael \par \uc2 854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Michael Renvillard was backstage at a New York City festival featuring Alicia Keys and Hugh Jackman when the longtime Lego representative noticed something odd. Strangers were bypassing celebrities and approaching him.\par \par \uc2 "They were wondering if I had one of those funny business cards," he recalls.\par \par \uc2 Within minutes, he had emptied his pocket of a dozen or so tiny Lego figures designed to look like him -- with his name and the company logo on the chest and his phone number and email on the back.\par \par \uc2 For years the Danish toy company has made customized minifigures for managers and other employees who deal with the public to use as business cards. Representatives of the \uc2 Lego Group\uc2  and \uc2 Lego Foundation\uc2 , for which Renvillard worked, handed them out at conferences and in meetings.\par \par \uc2 Obsessive Lego collectors, of course, began obsessing. Popular or rare versions of the tiny plastic employees can sell for as much as $1,000, according to some collectors. Renvillard's was listed on \uc2 eBay\uc2  this month for $275.\par \par \uc2 Stefano Debreri, a collector from Rome with a day job in finance, says he keeps most of his roughly 150 employee minifigures in a glass display case at home. He isn't in it for the money, he says. "I'm a romantic," he explains. "I collect for my pleasure."\par \par \uc2 Now, a little-noticed move in company headquarters is rocking this tiny collectibles market. \uc2 Lego\uc2  is moving away from the iconic plastic calling cards. Since last year, distribution of the minifigures has been limited to certain senior executives, a \uc2 Lego Group\uc2  spokesman said. He cited rapid growth in \uc2 Lego\uc2 's workforce and a corresponding jump in employee requests for the cards.\par \par \uc2 "Continuing to make them available to all colleagues, who are already connecting digitally, became a substantial and complex use of company resources," he said.\par \par \uc2 Among adult fans of Lego, who refer to themselves as AFOLs, employee minifigures are a niche interest. The collectors who focus on them often know each other personally, having met at fan conferences or traded the figures through online forums.\par \par \uc2 Most say they aren't in competition, though many keep track of how their collections stack up. Jesper Andersen, a Danish Lego fan who believes he once had continental Europe's largest collection, says he is looking to catch up after two rivals bested him while he was distracted with his family's move to a new house.\par \par \uc2 "I'm hoping to get back in the game," he says, acknowledging that it may be an uphill battle. One rival collector has friends who work at Lego, and the other is an employee himself.\par \par \uc2 Lego\uc2 's minifigures date to the 1970s, when the company first produced plastic figures with yellow heads, bendable arms, and legs made from traditional Lego bricks. Later, the design was updated, and the figures became so popular they are sold separately from \uc2 Lego\uc2  sets.\par \par \uc2 Employees say the company began turning them into business cards more than a decade ago. Staffers could order different styles, specifying facial expression and hairstyle. Some sport beards and glasses.\par \par \uc2 Jesper Vilstrup, a longtime Lego employee who left in 2022, says his raven-haired minifigure started drawing comments after his real hair began to gray. He didn't change the hair, he says, but he once ordered a bigger smile.\par \par \uc2 Collectors give considerable thought to how best to approach an employee. Some say they wait until a business card is offered because they don't want to seem too eager or make an employee uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 "I try to have a kind of connection," says Lluis Gibert, a tech worker and lifelong Lego fan from Barcelona, Spain, who has about 100 employee minifigures.\par \par \uc2 Gibert says he snagged many of them during his years editing a Lego fan magazine, when he often interviewed company employees. Like other collectors, he says, he made a knockoff version to use as his own card -- and for trading with \uc2 Lego\uc2  employees and other fans at \uc2 Lego\uc2  conventions.\par \par \uc2 Marc-Andre Bazergui, a Lego fan in Montreal, refers to his collection of about two dozen employee minifigures as his "mini Lego LinkedIn."\par \par \uc2 One of his most prized is of former Lego Chief Executive \uc2 Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen\uc2  -- a member of the family that controls the company -- which he got at a fan event. When he lined up with other fans a year or two later to get another, he says, Kristiansen seemed to remember him. "He said, 'You're not getting another one,'" recalls Bazergui.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, \uc2 Lego\uc2  moved to protect its intellectual property by informing the adult fan community they weren't allowed to put their own logos, sometimes using printing shops, on the figurines.\par \par \uc2 Michael Friedrichs, a collector in Germany, says he retired his personalized minifigures after the policy change because he didn't want to get into trouble or offend anyone.\par \par \uc2 When his parents found out what his collection might be worth, he says, they advised him to cash in.\par \par \uc2 "Get the money and buy something better," he says they told him. "Buy something cool."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240917ek9h0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
