\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Freezes Hiring, Delays Pay Increases\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 415 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said it would freeze hiring and delay pay increases for its salaried workforce as the jet maker grapples with financial fallout from a strike launched Friday by its largest union.\par \par \uc2 The company announced the cost-saving measures in a memo to staff on Monday and said it would also cut back orders from suppliers for its 737, 767 and 777 jets. The memo said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  also was considering temporary furloughs for employees and executives.\par \par \uc2 A strike could cost the manufacturer some $500 million a week, according to one analyst estimate. The company's operations had been burning about $1 billion a month before the strike, and credit-ratings firms warned they may downgrade \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This strike jeopardizes our recovery in a significant way, and we must take necessary actions to preserve cash and safeguard our shared future," \uc2 Boeing\uc2  finance chief \uc2 Brian West\uc2  said in Monday's memo.\par \par \uc2 The jet maker's 33,000-member machinists union walked out Friday after rejecting a labor deal struck between the union's leaders and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executives. The contract offered 25% wage increases over four years, bolstered retirement benefits, lowered healthcare costs and committed \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to building planes in the unionized Pacific Northwest. Union leaders were seeking a 40% pay raise over four years.\par \par \uc2 Executives cited \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's debt load and ongoing issues in pleas to workers ahead of Thursday's contract vote.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders, who unanimously approved the tentative contract rejected by the rank and file, are surveying members to determine priorities as they prepare to restart negotiations with the company on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers have said the proposed wage increases were too small after a decade of stagnating pay and the rising cost of living in the Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 About 94% of members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  chapter voted to reject the contract.\par \par \uc2 The union's members had never rejected a contract recommended by its leaders. Before their last deal, in 2008, negotiations led to a 57-day strike that stymied production and cost the company an estimated $100 million a day.\par \par \uc2 "Our confidence radiates from every picket line as we seize control of our future," the union's bargaining committee said in a note to members Friday. "We are ready for the long haul."\par \par \uc2 The company is eliminating all first and business-class air travel, including for executives. It also is pausing spending on outside consultants.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Alison Sider contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240917ek9h0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
