\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  Finds Unlikely Ally: Pittsburgh Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kris Maher, Bob Tita and Aaron Zitner \par \uc2 1,093 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEST MIFFLIN, Pa. -- Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have found agreement on one issue: opposing the $14.1 billion sale of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , one of America's most storied corporations, to Nippon Steel of Japan.\par \par \uc2 But outside Pittsburgh, in Monongahela River valley towns where steel is still made, some workers and officials say the rhetoric is disconnected from what is happening on the ground.\par \par \uc2 "I would bet that none of the national politicians have seen what I've seen and have not talked to these local workers," said West Mifflin Mayor Chris Kelly, who works at a desk in his garage beside a plaque reading, "Hours: Anytime." His town's \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  mill employs 800 workers who flatten glowing slabs of steel into sheet steel that is wound into giant coils. "This is nothing but politics," he added.\par \par \uc2 The potential sale looks different to many who battle blight and population loss after decades of plant closings, including by \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 . Some say fears of foreign ownership are overblown and the deal with deep-pocketed \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  offers the best chance to keep steel industry jobs in the region, and communities from being erased.\par \par \uc2 As Trump and Harris crisscross the nation's biggest swing state, opposing the deal has come to signal their support for domestic manufacturing and concern for working-class voters.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, who has been expected to block the deal, now appears to be delaying that decision, according to a person familiar with the matter. Biden believes it is "vital that \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  is to remain an American steel company" and awaits a national-security review, a White House spokeswoman said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Harris and Trump have said \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  should remain in domestic hands. All four senators from Pennsylvania and Ohio, including Sen. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who lives across the street from a \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  plant, have also opposed the merger.\par \par \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2 ' leaders have fought the deal since its December announcement. The union, backing a rival offer from Ohio-based \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2 , contends \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  could reduce production at \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  plants and substitute cheaper steel imported from its mills elsewhere, particularly India. It also worries about \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  honoring its existing labor contract.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Dave Burritt\uc2  recently escalated the stakes, threatening to close the company's Mon Valley Works -- three Pittsburgh-area plants employing more than 3,000 workers -- if the sale collapses. Burritt added \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's corporate offices would likely relocate near a mill in Arkansas if Mon Valley closes.\par \par \uc2 This raises a once-unthinkable possibility: For the first time in more than a century, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  would no longer have a presence in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, the mayor of West Mifflin, and other local officials are asking Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro to get the Biden administration to hear their concerns. A spokesman said the governor has been communicating with all parties involved and his priority is protecting Pennsylvania workers.\par \par \uc2 For Kelly, the effort transcends politics: He was the police chief in nearby Homestead when \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  closed a large mill there 40 years ago. Residents' despair from the closure, he said, caused an uptick in suicides and addictions.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  and \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  have intensified lobbying. Nippon Vice Chairman \uc2 Takahiro Mori\uc2  recently met with U.S. regulators. Ads running in Pittsburgh urge they "keep steel jobs in steel city."\par \par \uc2 The industry's realities are stark in the Monongahela River valley, where much of America's steel was once made. In the 1980s, when old mills succumbed to competition from foreign steelmakers and more efficient domestic ones, entire buildings were carted off or left to rust.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  has pledged to invest $2.7 billion in \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's older plants, including Mon Valley Works. This comes after \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  scrapped its own plan in 2021 to invest more than $1 billion there to upgrade production equipment and improve air emissions, a problem drawing millions of dollars in fines over the past decade.\par \par \uc2 In Braddock, the Edgar Thomson plant is among the world's oldest mills, producing steel from liquid iron heated in blast furnaces.\par \par \uc2 Braddock Mayor Delia Lennon-Winstead backs the deal and said presidential candidates who oppose it should provide another solution. "Do they actually walk these streets and live in this area?" she said. "Do they know the devastation of closing the mill, what it would do to this area?"\par \par \uc2 Clairton Mayor Rich Lattanzi, who retired after 30 years as a welder and pipe fitter at West Mifflin's plant, said he supports the deal. He thinks union leadership will eventually come around. "It's not going to be love at first sight," he said. Lattanzi said his town of 6,200, down from about 20,000 in 1950, receives 30% of its tax revenue from \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's Clairton Coke Works, North America's largest coking coal plant. Its closure, he said, would "take us off the map."\par \par \uc2 Don Furko, 50, a mechanical maintenance planner at Clairton's plant, joined \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  in 2000, shortly after his father retired from there. After bargaining alongside union leadership, he trusts their opposition to the deal. "I'm going to back what they say."\par \par \uc2 Others, such as Jason Zugai, 47, vice president of the steelworkers local for West Mifflin's plant, disagree with top union brass. "We're not on the same page as the leaders," he said. "They'd still have jobs if the plant gets shut down."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Alexander Ward and Andrew Restuccia contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 A Steelmaker\par \par \uc2 And Its City\par \par \uc2 Pittsburgh's bond with \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  runs deep. Founded in 1901, the company was among the first to reach $1 billion in market value, and in 1943 its companywide workforce exceeded 340,000, studies show. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  jobs supported generations of Pittsburgh families. Today the company employs about 14,000 people in North America, including some 4,000 in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 The company's legacy permeates Pittsburgh through names of parks, museums and libraries -- and on the \uc2 Pittsburgh Steelers\uc2 ' football helmets, which feature the three-diamond design created by \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Steel Tower is downtown's tallest building, but today symbolizes the rising prominence of robotics, biomedical research and AI, driven by \uc2 Carnegie Mellon University\uc2  and the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2 . It now bears the logo of healthcare provider and insurer UPMC.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240917ek9h0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
