\par\par \b \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's Fast-Charger Growth Slows Down\b0\par\par\uc2 By Becky Peterson \par \uc2 951 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's expansion of its Supercharger network has slowed in recent months, after widespread layoffs in April gutted the team responsible for installing new electric-car chargers and stations.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has been trying to rebuild the group, including rehiring some employees previously let go, the number of new Supercharger ports opened in May through August fell 28% from the same period a year earlier, according to EV analytics firm EVAdoption.\par \par \uc2 For the first eight months of this year, that number was down 11% from 2023, the firm's data show. The decline comes as the electric-car maker has received millions of dollars in government funding this summer to install new chargers in states including Maryland and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is under pressure to expand its network of fast chargers and open it to a wider array of drivers, including those with non-\uc2 Tesla\uc2  vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Rivian Automotive\uc2  already have access to \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's plug-in technology and several other car companies have forged agreements to use the chargers for their own customers, a shift that could help broaden the once-exclusive service.\par \par \uc2 The rollout to other automakers has run into some obstacles this year, owing in part to software challenges and holdups distributing the plug-in adapters needed by non-\uc2 Tesla\uc2  customers.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Tesla\uc2  actually has one of its biggest challenges in front of it since the Supercharger was first released," said Nick Nigro, founder of the research and consulting group Atlas Public Policy.\par \par \uc2 "What's important for them, in planning around where to allocate resources, is what's coming in the next 12 to 24 months -- that is millions of drivers," Nigro said.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  didn't return requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  posted on X that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is "opening a lot more Superchargers."\par \par \uc2 With 6,500 charging locations globally, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's Supercharger network is widely regarded as one of the industry's most robust and reliable services, and it has become a major selling point for buyers considering the electric-car brand.\par \par \uc2 The service's growth was disrupted this spring when \uc2 Tesla\uc2  abruptly let go of the entire Supercharger team and its head, Rebecca Tinucci, amid companywide layoffs, according to people familiar with the events.\par \par \uc2 The move threw the industry into chaos, pausing construction on some Supercharger sites that were under development and causing confusion among property owners who were in the middle of negotiations at the time of the job cuts.\par \par \uc2 Months later, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is trying to get back on track with a smaller team and new leadership of the group. Their task is the same as before -- to expand \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's network. Only now, they need to do it with fewer employees, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Musk has tried to reassure customers that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  would continue to build out the service, just at a slower pace for new locations. In May, he said the company plans to spend well over $500 million to improve the network with thousands of new chargers this year.\par \par \uc2 "That's just on new sites and expansions, not counting operations costs, which are much higher," Musk said.\par \par \uc2 The reality has been more complicated. For the first few weeks after the layoffs, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's partners and contractors struggled to reach their contacts at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , most of whom no longer worked at the company or had access to email.\par \par \uc2 By the end of May, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  brought back several senior members of the Supercharger team, including Max de Zegher, who leads the program, and a handful of managers who run it in North America.\par \par \uc2 Mike Snyder, a longtime executive at \uc2 Tesla\uc2  who had previously run its industrial-battery program, has since assumed oversight of the charging group, according to an organizational chart viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Construction on Supercharger stations remains active across the country, and in recent weeks, the team has continued rehiring some former workers, people involved in those conversations said.\par \par \uc2 A continued slowdown could have an impact on the charging experience for electric-vehicle customers across the industry.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in late 2023 agreed to make its charging service available to other automakers for the first time. The move also allowed \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to qualify for a share of the billions in federal dollars available for enlarging the country's EV charging infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 So far, the company has secured about $37 million in public funding to build 88 Supercharger stations in the U.S., making it one of the top recipients in a federal program to expand EV charging access, according to EVAdoption, which tracks the awards.\par \par \uc2 Some of that money was doled out via state programs after the layoffs. In August, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  received $2.9 million to install six charging sites in Arizona. A month earlier, it got $1.8 million from Maryland to install its fast chargers, which can allow EVs to repower in about 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's need for expansion is coming into sharper focus with more automakers poised to join the network. Already, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  drivers are worried about congestion at existing stations and the potential for longer wait times.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the company opened its charger network to Ford and \uc2 Rivian\uc2  drivers. It also had plans to expand access to \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Volvo Cars\uc2  and Polestar by this past spring, but its website now says those additions are coming soon.\par \par \uc2 "That was one downside that came as a result of opening up that network to the non-\uc2 Tesla\uc2  owners," said Brent Gruber, executive director of the EV practice at \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 , which studies charging satisfaction. "They no longer had access to that exclusive club. Now they're sharing it with other owners."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich509\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7635
