\par\par \b \uc2 A Strike At \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Adds to New CEO's Challenges\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A tough job just got much tougher for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's new CEO.\par \par \uc2 When the jet maker's largest union went on strike Friday, the walkout compounded the list of problems facing \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , who took \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's top job five weeks ago. Among them are rapid cash burn, a struggling supply base and a manufacturing-quality crisis.\par \par \uc2 Hours after the walkout, debt-ratings firms warned that a prolonged work stoppage would prompt them to downgrade \uc2 Boeing\uc2  debt into junk status. With more than $45 billion in net debt, a ratings hit would drive up borrowing costs and hamper fundraising efforts.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  shares on Friday fell nearly 4%, nearing a two-year low.\par \par \uc2 The jet maker burned through more than $1 billion a month in the year's first half and warned in July that the company would burn between $5 billion and $10 billion in cash this year. Largely to blame is \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's slowed production of the 737 as the company works to address quality issues after a door plug blew off in midair on an \uc2 Alaska Air\uc2  flight in January.\par \par \uc2 The company is struggling with production slowdowns on other models, too, because of supplier shortages and other issues. Its defense business, which makes F-15 jet fighters and Chinook helicopters for the Pentagon, also is unprofitable. \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Starliner spacecraft faces an uncertain future after technical issues left two astronauts stuck on the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of machinists walked off the job shortly after midnight Pacific time Friday, rejecting a labor deal struck between the union's leaders and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executives. The tentative contract had offered a 25% wage increase over four years.\par \par \uc2 The strike was overwhelmingly approved by the 33,000 members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  union chapter.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  finance chief \uc2 Brian West\uc2  said the company will work on a second offer. He said the company was initially pleased with the outcome of talks given that union leaders unanimously signed off on the first deal.\par \par \uc2 He said Ortberg spent last week visiting factories and is focused on coming up with a new deal workers would approve. The parties are scheduled to resume discussions on Tuesday -- this time with a federal mediator.\par \par \uc2 "His priorities are to reset, re-engage and rebuild," West said Friday at an investor conference.\par \par \uc2 Ortberg is short on time.\par \par \uc2 Ratings firm \uc2 Fitch\uc2  said a one- or two-week strike likely wouldn't affect \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's rating, but anything longer could. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cited cash-flow concerns in placing all of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's credit ratings on review for possible downgrades. Both firms currently rate \uc2 Boeing\uc2  one notch above junk status.\par \par \uc2 Each bond-ratings drop would cost \uc2 Boeing\uc2  an extra $100 million in annual interest payments, Jefferies analyst \uc2 Sheila Kahyaoglu\uc2  estimated. The company has paid more than $2 billion in interest in the past year.\par \par \uc2 West, speaking Friday, said the company was giving priority to its investment-grade rating.\par \par \uc2 Ortberg earlier this month took to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's factory floors, speaking and listening to workers there in hopes of avoiding a strike. He acknowledged in a letter to union members that \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's mistakes contributed to its current situation.\par \par \uc2 Jon Holden, the union chapter's president, said Ortberg was in a tough position as a newcomer trying to mitigate years of animosity between the union and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  leadership.\par \par \uc2 Longtime machinists are angry about union concessions over the past 16 years that have eroded retirement and health benefits. Recent hires, meanwhile, point to the ever-increasing cost of living and the company's stagnant starting wages.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to make up for 16 years," Holden said. "And I think that's the position he was in."\par \par \uc2 A prolonged stoppage would hit suppliers, which had just begun to recover from shutdowns caused by the pandemic and the 20-month grounding of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's 737 MAX after fatal crashes in 2018 and 2019. Shutdowns have lingering effects, particularly on smaller parts makers that are forced to cut jobs when orders dry up and then must rehire and retrain workers.\par \par \uc2 To avoid that dynamic, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  largely refrained from cutting parts orders despite its own production slowdown. That means the company is holding billions of dollars in inventory of unused parts.\par \par \uc2 West, the finance chief, said that as soon as the strike started, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  told suppliers to stop sending parts if the company had sufficient inventory.\par \par \uc2 "If you're not behind, we have safety stock. Don't deliver anymore," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ortberg, an aerospace veteran who ran one of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's big suppliers, \uc2 Rockwell Collins\uc2 , succeeded former Chief Executive \uc2 Dave Calhoun\uc2 , who said in March that he would step down. During the monthslong search, several high-profile candidates turned down the chance to run the company.\par \par \uc2 GE Aerospace\uc2  CEO \uc2 Larry Culp\uc2 , widely considered a natural for the job, declined \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's request to consider taking over.\par \par \uc2 David Gitlin\uc2 , the current chief executive of manufacturer \uc2 Carrier Global\uc2 , also removed himself from the list of potential contenders.\par \par \uc2 RBC\uc2  analyst \uc2 Ken Herbert\uc2  said Ortberg must resolve the strike in a week to avoid tarnishing his reputation. The strike pressures \uc2 Boeing\uc2  leaders, he said, but shouldn't derail Ortberg's long-term efforts to make changes at the company.\par \par \uc2 "Not the start expected for new CEO Ortberg," he said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240916ek9g0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
