\par\par \b \uc2 The New Workplace (A Special Report): Management Top 250 --- The Little-Known Company That Gives Its Employees a Real Voice: Most of the top 10 companies in our rankings of employee engagement are household names. Then there's \uc2 Prologis\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rick Wartzman and Kelly Tang \par \uc2 1,436 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Like most corporations, \uc2 Prologis\uc2  has festooned its website with lots of slogans, such as this one tucked beneath a photograph of smiling colleagues gathered around a conference table, laptops open, pens in hand: "Our people drive our success. That's why we've built a culture of personal and professional empowerment."\par \par \uc2 Unlike most corporations, the logistics real-estate giant has figured out how to turn this platitude into practice, the rhetoric into results.\par \par \uc2 "We work at it religiously," says \uc2 Hamid Moghadam\uc2 , the chairman and CEO of \uc2 Prologis\uc2 , which has about 2,700 employees.\par \par \uc2 The proof can be found in a gauge of corporate effectiveness devised by the Drucker Institute at Claremont Graduate University.\par \par \uc2 Based on the core principles of the late management scholar \uc2 Peter Drucker\uc2 , who defined effectiveness as "doing the right things well," the measure examines five dimensions of performance: customer satisfaction, innovation, social responsibility, employee engagement and development, and financial strength.\par \par \uc2 In all, we take in 34 metrics to assess how companies compare with each other in these five categories, as well as in their overall effectiveness, using standardized scores with a typical range of 0 to 100 and a mean of 50.\par \par \uc2 The statistical model forms the foundation of the Management Top 250, an annual ranking produced in partnership with The Wall Street Journal. Bendable Labs, a private firm, works with the institute to make the calculations and interpret them.\par \par \uc2 The Management Top 250 for 2023, published in December, was drawn from a bigger universe of 794 large, publicly traded U.S. corporations.\par \par \uc2 In his writings, Drucker decried the way that most companies would talk about how their people were their most important asset but then fail to use them to the fullest. "The few enterprises that have been able to tap this unused reservoir of human ability and attitude," he asserted, "have achieved spectacular increases in productivity and output."\par \par \uc2 Given this, we wanted to see which companies -- out of the 465 for which we have data going back to 2019 -- have done best in the area of employee engagement and development over the past five years.\par \par \uc2 Notably, seven of the top 10 are tech firms, including \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . This doesn't surprise \uc2 Aaron Terrazas\uc2 , the chief economist at \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 , which supplies data for the Drucker rankings. The tech industry's high level of productivity and strong financial position give "these companies the ability to invest in their employee experience," he says.\par \par \uc2 The others are \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  -- and one company that is hardly a household name: \uc2 Prologis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Moghadam notes that \uc2 Prologis\uc2 's low profile can make it challenging at times to attract talent. "Nobody has heard of us," he says. "People don't relate to buildings, particularly warehouses. We're not developing high-rises with a designer architect."\par \par \uc2 Once \uc2 Prologis\uc2  has a chance to tell its story, however, there is plenty to showcase. For starters, it is the world's largest industrial property company. Some 3% of global GDP runs through its facilities. In turn, employees "know that what they do matters," Moghadam says.\par \par \uc2 The company, which prides itself on being innovative, has moved briskly into sustainable energy, offering on-site solar and other products and services that can help its customers meet their carbon-reduction goals. That, Moghadam says, brings "a lot of excitement for our people" -- a phenomenon that we've documented in earlier research.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of everything, meanwhile, is the work environment that Moghadam set out to create in 1983, when he co-founded the real-estate investment firm that would morph through acquisition into the current iteration of \uc2 Prologis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some of it, coincidentally, grew out of his own reading of \uc2 Peter Drucker\uc2  when he was a teenager living in Tehran. A self-described leadership nerd from that young age, he devoured the Drucker books an uncle had recommended. Moghadam's main takeaway: Well-managed companies give employees, regardless of rank or title, real authority and responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Today at \uc2 Prologis\uc2 , that approach "permeates throughout the organization," says Nathaalie Carey, the senior vice president of human resources. "You're asked to be a risk-taker."\par \par \uc2 Art Makris, who spent 25 years at \uc2 Duke Realty\uc2 , which \uc2 Prologis\uc2  acquired in 2022 for $23 billion, says he has found the encouragement to constantly try new things. "I've never been told no," says Makris, the head of customer-led development for the Americas -- though he has definitely been told to sharpen or refine his ideas.\par \par \uc2 Makris stresses that everyone at \uc2 Prologis\uc2  is expected to speak up internally as well as with customers -- even the biggest -- regardless of whether they're a seasoned veteran like he is or a fresh-out-of-school associate. "It's safe" to suggest things, he says, which helps each person on a project feel ownership.\par \par \uc2 In its own employee engagement program, which uses a survey tool from \uc2 Qualtrics\uc2 , \uc2 Prologis\uc2  doesn't rate uniformly high. When it comes to how its workers feel about job stress or information sharing, for example, it is close to average. But among the many areas in which the company does excel are these: "I have the authority I need to do my job" and "I'm empowered to make decisions to best serve my customers." In both cases, 85% to 90% of \uc2 Prologis\uc2  employees respond favorably, which is significantly above \uc2 Qualtrics\uc2 's global benchmark.\par \par \uc2 Having this kind of autonomy, coupled with support from leadership, is "a consistent and persistent driver of motivation," says Benjamin Granger, chief workplace psychologist at \uc2 Qualtrics\uc2 . "It's why you enjoy getting up and going to work every day."\par \par \uc2 Michele Zanini, the co-author with Gary Hamel of "Humanocracy: Creating Organizations as Amazing as the People Inside Them," says he often encounters executives who are confident they can foster employee engagement just by issuing pronouncements, when in fact the effort has to go much deeper than that.\par \par \uc2 "You don't need a fresh coat of paint," Zanini says. "You need gene therapy. It's how you organize work -- who actually gets agency and authority and voice and resources."\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Prologis\uc2 , all of these dynamics are on display at the company's Monday investment committee meeting. The stakes are huge, with $500 million in potential projects typically on the line every week.\par \par \uc2 The panel that makes the final decisions consists of Moghadam, the chief financial officer and others from the C-suite. Also on the committee are three middle managers who rotate quarterly -- a valuable way to expose up-and-comers to the fundamentals of the business.\par \par \uc2 The cross-functional teams that are pitching submit their proposals the previous Wednesday. That gives the investment committee time to ask questions, with replies due over the weekend. By the time Monday rolls around, everyone feels well prepared. "We don't ambush people," Moghadam says.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Rutherford, a leasing officer in New Jersey who joined \uc2 Prologis\uc2  in 2020, has appeared before the investment committee more than half a dozen times. "It's always a little nerve-racking," she says. But even though she is surrounded by higher-ups, she says she never hesitates to give her candid views on market trends.\par \par \uc2 "Any question I've been asked, I always feel trusted," Rutherford says. "I'm the local expert."\par \par \uc2 For his part, Moghadam goes last so that he doesn't prematurely sway the conversation. When he does weigh in -- and this holds true beyond these Monday meetings -- nobody has to wonder where he stands.\par \par \uc2 "He's direct, and people love it," says Jennifer Nelson, the senior vice president for global communications. "That's part of the secret sauce."\par \par \uc2 In building culture, consistency helps, too. The median tenure for the CEO of an S&P 500 company is about five years, according to \uc2 Equilar\uc2  -- a contrast from Moghadam's decades at the helm.\par \par \uc2 Along the way, he has accumulated hundreds of millions of dollars in \uc2 Prologis\uc2  stock. He says he has never sold a share other than expiring options that had to be exercised or to fund charitable gifts to his alma maters, Stanford and MIT. "People know I'm all in," Moghadam says.\par \par \uc2 As the numbers attest, so are they.\par \par \uc2 Rick Wartzman is co-president and Kelly Tang is chief data scientist at Bendable Labs. They are also both senior research fellows at Claremont Graduate University. They can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240916ek9g00004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
