\par\par \b \uc2 The New Workplace (A Special Report) --- Your Company Is Watching You. And Doing It All Wrong: Employers often feel like have to monitor workers. There are bad ways to do it and not-as-bad ways.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Brodsky \par \uc2 843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When it comes to companies monitoring employees, there is a fundamental mismatch: Companies want to do it and employees generally don't like it. After all, who wants somebody looking over their shoulder?\par \par \uc2 So how do companies monitor employees in a way that minimizes the resentment?\par \par \uc2 Many companies believe they have no choice but to monitor their employees, especially in an era of remote work. Bosses say they want to make sure workers are being as productive as possible and aren't slacking off, absent in-person supervision. According to \uc2 Gartner\uc2 , 71% of employees are now digitally monitored in some way, up from 30% before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The issue is that many of these companies are misguided: There is no substantial body of evidence saying that monitoring is better than not monitoring at all. Research, in fact, suggests that monitoring doesn't do much except stress out employees.\par \par \uc2 But as long as companies feel they have to keep an eye on their employees, there are bad ways to do it and there are not-as-bad ways. These latter strategies won't make people love being watched, or necessarily make them more productive. But they can help employees feel better about the process -- as well as about their work and employer.\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at some of the best approaches that I have found in my research, as well as in studies done by others.\par \par \uc2 Be transparent\par \par \uc2 Too often, companies monitor --- doing things like logging website visits or even spying on employees through webcams -- but don't let employees know. That is a mistake. Employees will inevitably find out. The better approach is to tell employees that they are being monitored and, just as important, why the company is doing it. This makes monitoring seem more honest and less sinister, and it becomes easier for employees to buy in.\par \par \uc2 Imagine that a consulting firm starts tracking employee activity. If it just tells workers that it is keeping an eye on them -- without any other context -- the staff will likely get edgy and suspicious. But employees would likely feel a lot better if the company explains that it wants clients to feel more secure that billable hours are accurate.\par \par \uc2 One study that looked at 257 university call-center employees showed how important it is for managers to properly explain monitoring -- and how it is being used to assess workers. Employees who thought their manager's explanation was adequate felt significantly greater fairness and trust in their manager. And, in turn, they were also more satisfied with their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Make the data available\par \par \uc2 It isn't enough to tell employees they are being watched. Companies should give them access to the data that is collected. Again, transparency will make workers more comfortable with surveillance and less suspicious of their employers. In a survey of 800 German respondents, researchers found that they were significantly more critical of monitoring systems in which only the employer -- but not the employees -- had access to the data.\par \par \uc2 Frame it smartly\par \par \uc2 It is also important to present monitoring in the right light. Employees often feel as though companies use tracking data as a "gotcha" to punish them. Instead, companies should show workers how the collected data can help them do their jobs better -- such as tracking the impact of their work on clients or how much time they spend in meetings versus work tasks.\par \par \uc2 A study of 330 sales and customer-service employees in a Midwestern telecommunications organization bears this out. The more that employees thought monitoring systems were helping their growth, the more satisfaction and commitment they felt.\par \par \uc2 But companies shouldn't take transparency to the extreme. Consider a study of 108 students correcting entries in a database. In cases where students were frequently reminded they were being monitored, they reported feeling less in control of their work.\par \par \uc2 Give employees a choice\par \par \uc2 Employees may also respond badly -- in attitude and performance -- if they have less control over the process. In one experiment, some people doing a data-entry and proofing task were able to turn off monitoring altogether. Their accuracy improved by 22% compared to those who had no control over monitoring.\par \par \uc2 Employers might worry that workers would abuse that power to temporarily turn off monitoring. Yet only four participants actually did so. Although these researchers didn't study this specifically, these findings align with a substantial body of research on impression management, which has found that people generally realize that being seen putting effort into their work will make them look good, and "hiding" their efforts too often may be received negatively.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Andrew Brodsky is an assistant professor of management at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  and author of the forthcoming book "PING: The Secrets of Successful Virtual Communication." He can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240916ek9g00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
