\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: New \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO Faces Early Challenge --- Union to vote on strike that would stop most production and deal financial blow\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's new boss, \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , urged union members not to go on a strike that would halt most of its airplane production and further upend the aerospace giant's struggling manufacturing operations.\par \par \uc2 The company's largest union was set to vote late Thursday on a tentative agreement that calls for a 25% pay increase and commits \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to building its next plane in the unionized Pacific Northwest. Workers have taken to social media and inundated union leaders to protest the deal.\par \par \uc2 "I ask you not to sacrifice the opportunity to secure our future together, because of the frustrations of the past," Ortberg said in a message to workers. The chief executive said he has been speaking to workers at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's main assembly plants in the area. "I hope you will choose the bright future ahead, but I also know there are employees considering another path -- and it's one where no one wins."\par \par \uc2 In stopping much of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's production, the strike would deal a financial blow at a time the aerospace giant is bleeding cash and piling up debt in the wake of January's \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  accident in which a door plug blew off a 737 MAX jetliner in midair.\par \par \uc2 A prolonged work stoppage could further strain the industry's supply chain and exacerbate jet shortages for airlines struggling to meet resurgent travel demand.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the 33,000-member \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  union chapter had reached a deal with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  on Sunday following weeks of tense negotiations. The contract covers workers who build the 737, 767 and 777 jets in the Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreement calls for a 25% pay increase over four years. It would bolster retirement benefits, lower healthcare costs and commit \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to building its next plane in the unionized Pacific Northwest, a crucial union demand after the company set up a nonunion plant in South Carolina to build the 787 more than a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 But the deal falls well short of what the union had said was a hard line, including a 40% pay increase over four years. It might not win over a membership irate over years of concessions and \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's shift to nonunion labor, as well as what some see as the company's self-inflicted financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders and company officials have had less than a week to pitch the deal. A majority of IAM members have to vote to ratify the contract. Even if the contract is rejected, it would take effect unless two-thirds of members vote in favor of a strike.\par \par \uc2 Besieged by workers upset about the deal, Jon Holden, president of the \uc2 IAM union\uc2  chapter, said this week that he anticipated the company's rank-and-file would reject the deal and vote to strike.\par \par \uc2 "We have achieved everything we could in bargaining, short of a strike," Holden said in a message to members days before the vote. "We recommended acceptance because we can't guarantee we can achieve more in a strike."\par \par \uc2 Ortberg, who took over \uc2 Boeing\uc2  last month, met with Holden in the final hours of negotiations and promised that the company would assemble \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's next new plane in the Seattle region.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Pope\uc2 , chief of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial unit, told workers the company is giving all it can given its mounting debt pile, which topped $60 billion at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 Pope, in her message, said the company would not only assemble the next new \uc2 Boeing\uc2  airplane in the Pacific Northwest, but also had committed to building and assembling all major components in the region.\par \par \uc2 "In past negotiations, the thinking was we should hold something back so we can ratify the contract on a second vote," Pope said to union members. "Let me be clear: We did not hold back with an eye on a second vote."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240913ek9d00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
