\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Trims Videogame Staff --- Layoffs of 650 mark the latest round for game employees since \uc2 Activision\uc2  buy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah E. Needleman \par \uc2 525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is laying off approximately 650 employees from its videogame business, the tech giant's latest move to cut costs after its $75 billion acquisition of \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs affect mostly workers in corporate and support roles and are being done to "organize our business for long-term success," said \uc2 Phil Spencer\uc2 , chief executive of Microsoft Gaming, in a company email Thursday viewed by The Wall Street Journal. No studios will be closed, nor will any games, devices or experiences be canceled, he said.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's game-division layoffs are part of a significant downsizing in the global games industry. While demand for interactive entertainment shot up during the pandemic -- following decades of mostly steady growth -- consumer spending declined in 2022 and has rebounded only modestly since then.\par \par \uc2 The move also comes as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has been investing heavily in artificial intelligence, including more than $13 billion in funding for ChatGPT maker \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . The tech giant said it spent $19 billion on capital expenditures and equipment in the three months that ended in June. The figure is the same amount the company spent in 2019 for the entire year.\par \par \uc2 The gaming-division cuts Thursday follow the Xbox maker's decision to let go of roughly 1,900 workers from the unit at the start of the year and the closure of three of its studios in May.\par \par \uc2 In January 2023, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  laid off 10,000 employees across all its businesses, including Xbox personnel. The company then gained around 10,000 employees with the October acquisition of \uc2 Activision\uc2 , the biggest deal in its history.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, game companies overall have let go more than 11,500 employees, according to an online tally of termination announcements and news reports compiled by Farhan Noor, a technical artist in California. That figure compares with 10,500 in all of 2023 and 8,500 in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  doesn't break out game-related revenue, but analysts and researchers say its Xbox machines have long trailed sales of \uc2 Sony Group\uc2 's PlayStations and \uc2 Nintendo\uc2 's Switch and other consoles. Game software and accessories sales typically correlate with demand for game hardware.\par \par \uc2 To catch up with its rivals, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has been working to increase the number of subscribers to its Game Pass service, which gives users access to several hundred games, which can be streamed to consoles, PCs, smartphones and tablets. The company last disclosed in February that the number of subscribers had reached 34 million. In July, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  raised the price of Game Pass.\par \par \uc2 A major test of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's strategy with Game Pass will come in late October, when the company plans to release \uc2 Activision\uc2 's next Call of Duty game on the service the same day it goes on sale a la carte. The move is risky because consumers could end up paying \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  less for the new installment of the shooter series -- one of the most successful entertainment properties ever -- than it would have made with the traditional approach of only selling it outright.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240913ek9d0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
