\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Boosts Pay for Its Drivers Following Threats to Unionize\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung and Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Your \uc2 Amazon\uc2  driver is up for a raise.\par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  is putting billions of dollars toward the drivers that deliver its packages following union organizing activity among such workers.\par \par \uc2 The company said it is investing about $2 billion into its delivery services program this year, and that the money will result in average national pay for drivers delivering \uc2 Amazon\uc2  parcels to reach nearly $22 an hour, a 7% increase from last year.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has been delivering most of its own packages with the help of small businesses around the country since 2018. The average pay for drivers was $20.50 last year.\par \par \uc2 These drivers are hired by what \uc2 Amazon\uc2  calls Delivery Service Partners, or small local businesses that hire drivers and decide their salaries. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it can dictate only what the minimum pay for drivers is. The minimum pay for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  drivers across the country would range from $19.50 to $25.25 an hour this year, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  added that it also is introducing an app that the businesses can offer that will allow drivers easier access to their accrued wages without having to wait for payday.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said this is the highest investment in the program since it started in 2018. Last year it invested more than $840 million into the program.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said part of the investment includes technology updates that simplify delivery instructions that customers provide in preset fields, to make it easier for drivers, especially in large apartment communities.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's investment follows union organizing activity and changes to how labor officials view its drivers.\par \par \uc2 Officials with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  have recently ruled that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is a joint employer of drivers who are contracted to deliver packages for the company at facilities in Palmdale, Calif., and Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 The rulings could change how the agency views \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's relationship with drivers and could force \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to bargain with those workers if they unionize.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , one of America's largest labor unions, has led strikes of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers across the nation. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have focused part of their efforts on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  drivers. The Amazon Labor Union, a labor group that successfully unionized \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees at a warehouse in New York, recently voted to join the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  uses a similar model, joining with independent contractors to deliver ground parcels. These small businesses purchase and own routes, vehicles and other equipment, as well as hire drivers.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  signed a contract with the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  that it says maintained \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers as the highest-paid delivery drivers in the country, with the average top rate at $49 an hour. The drivers previously made around $42 an hour after they have been employed for four years.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  still uses \uc2 UPS\uc2  and the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  to deliver some of its parcels.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240913ek9d00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
