\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 How \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  Defines Free Speech\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats are campaigning on "freedom," but it pays to watch how they define it. In Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's Minnesota, it doesn't include free speech for employers.\par \par \uc2 A federal court next week will hear a challenge to a 2023 Minnesota law that bars employers from discussing "religious or political matters" at mandatory meetings. The latter includes elections, government regulations, laws and whether to join or support a union. This seems to mean that employers can't criticize Mr. Walz, advocate for pro-business policies or discourage unionization.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say employees shouldn't be obliged to attend "captive audience" meetings in which employers express their views about unions or government policies. But workers aren't captive. They're paid to listen. They can disregard their bosses' opinions or quit.\par \par \uc2 Employers argue in their lawsuit that the law burdens their First Amendment rights and is pre-empted by federal law. They will now have to watch their every word at company meetings. Mr. Walz crowed at a North America's Building Trades Unions legislative conference this spring that employers who violate the law "go to jail now."\par \par \uc2 He was exaggerating since the law doesn't carry criminal penalties. But the state can dun employers, and workers can sue them for damages. This chills the speech of employers, which is the point of the law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz claims the law is needed to protect workers from employer "coercion." But the National Labor Relations Act already forbids employers from punishing employees for supporting a union or engaging in other "protected concerted activity." It also permits employers to express "any views, argument, or opinion" about unions as long as their "expression contains no threat of reprisal or force or promise of benefit."\par \par \uc2 Fearing the state could lose the lawsuit, Minnesota Attorney General Keith Ellison says it should be dismissed because the state doesn't intend to enforce the law. Ergo, the plaintiffs lack legal standing to bring the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Walz doesn't sound like he plans to let employers off the hook. A month ago he told the American Federation of State, County, and Municipal Employees (Afscme) convention that the law "was the best thing to get sued over I ever said. We're going to continue to ban those meetings."\par \par \uc2 Democrats want to have it both ways by claiming the state doesn't plan to enforce the law in order to deny plaintiffs the right to challenge it. Yet they're keeping the law on the books with its threat of fines and worker lawsuits as a cudgel to coerce employers into self-censoring.\par \par \uc2 Democrats plan to do the same if they win control of Washington in November. A top priority will be passing the Pro Act, which lets unions bully employees and employers. The legislation would let the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  order employers who hold captive-audience meetings to bargain with a union. Workers could also sue employers for violating the law, and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  could dun corporate directors and officers.\par \par \uc2 Oh, and it would also ban right-to-work laws in 26 states that give workers the choice of whether to join a union. This is the antithesis of freedom.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240910ek9a00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
