\par\par \b \uc2 SEC Fines Seven Companies for Whistleblower-Protection Violations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mengqi Sun \par \uc2 323 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  has continued its crackdown on companies over language in their employment and separation agreements that could discourage whistleblowers from coming forward.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the SEC fined seven public companies -- in industries ranging from healthcare to software -- more than $3 million combined, alleging their employment, separation and other agreements contain confidentiality clauses that violated SEC whistleblower-protection rules, including requiring employees to waive their right to possible whistleblower monetary awards.\par \par \uc2 Among the companies, behavioral healthcare services provider \uc2 Acadia Healthcare\uc2  received the largest fine in Monday's settlement, agreeing to pay nearly $1.4 million in civil penalties. Software provider \uc2 AppFolio\uc2  followed, agreeing to pay $692,250 in civil fines, while fashion retailer a.k.a. Brands Holding said it would pay $399,750 and credit-reporting agency \uc2 TransUnion\uc2 , $312,000.\par \par \uc2 Also fined were \uc2 LSB Industries\uc2 , IDEX and \uc2 Smart for Life\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The SEC said the seven firms agreed to comply with whistleblower-protection rules going forward and have taken steps to remediate the issues, including by making changes to their relevant agreements.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Acadia Healthcare\uc2  said it "understands the importance of regulatory requirements and worked quickly to resolve this issue with the SEC."\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 TransUnion\uc2  said the settlement "reflects the seriousness with which we take our commitment to regulatory compliance," adding that the firm is making voluntary changes to ensure employees and contractors better understand their rights and responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for \uc2 AppFolio\uc2  and \uc2 a.k.a. Brands\uc2  didn't respond to requests to comment.\par \par \uc2 Monday's settlements are part of a sweep by the SEC over the past few years into companies' use of confidentiality provisions and nondisclosure clauses in their various employment contracts. A watershed moment came last September, when the SEC fined hedge fund D.E. Shaw $10 million.\par \par \uc2 The SEC recently expanded the investigation into companies' use of confidentiality agreements with clients.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240910ek9a0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
