\par\par \b \uc2 Companies Expand Paths to Using Virtual Reality in Training Programs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabelle Bousquette \par \uc2 972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More employees are strapping on virtual-reality headsets as the immersive technology becomes an increasingly common method for workforce training on a range of topics, from hardware maintenance to leadership and empathy.\par \par \uc2 Companies such as \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  have taken a slow but measured approach to a technology that has drawn extreme hype and extreme skepticism over the years as businesses have tested, piloted and pulled back on various uses.\par \par \uc2 "It's an interesting, but slower-than-expected journey. But for training -- I think VR will always be a good way to use it," said Johan Hellqvist, head of mobile and XR/3D at \uc2 Volvo Group\uc2 . The company uses VR to train employees on actions like replacing the battery on an electric truck.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  is using it to train drivers how to stack packages or handle certain situations in the field, such as a dog attack. Companies like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  are using it to train on so-called soft skills, such as how to show empathy when dealing with a frustrated customer.\par \par \uc2 Virtual reality only became commercially available to consumers and enterprises in the past 10 to 12 years, said \uc2 Gartner\uc2  analyst Tuong Nguyen. At the peak of its hype three years ago, companies said they were exploring a range of uses from VR meetings to buying ads in the metaverse.\par \par \uc2 Many of these failed to prove their value, as companies realized that \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Teams or Zoom could do the meeting job at a sliver of the cost of bulky headsets, Nguyen said. \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 ' Reality Labs division, in many ways seen as a barometer of the tech's adoption, has struggled to attract and retain users and incurred an operating loss of more than $16 billion in 2023.\par \par \uc2 But VR hardware and software have improved and become more affordable. Headsets are sleeker and less likely to give users motion sickness. Companies have developed broader content libraries for VR. With those developments, and years of trial and error, companies say virtual reality has the potential to augment and improve on real-world training.\par \par \uc2 "The way people learn is fundamentally changing, and VR is what is driving that change," said Brad Scoggin, co-founder and chief executive of ArborXR, which helps manage and integrate virtual reality devices and content and works with \uc2 UPS\uc2  and Volvo, among others. "Doing, say, harassment training in VR is much more effective than watching a video with actors," he said. "In VR, you can actually put a headset on and experience what it's like to be in somebody else's shoes."\par \par \uc2 Companies are doing a mix of creating their own VR training content and outsourcing it, and using headsets from a range of providers, including \uc2 Meta\uc2 , HTC, Pico, \uc2 Apple\uc2  and Lenovo.\par \par \uc2 It can still be expensive, with a minimum six-figure investment to get started -- regardless of whether companies are creating or outsourcing content, \uc2 Gartner\uc2 's Nguyen said. But enterprises say it still works out to be less expensive than traditional methods that require equipment and travel.\par \par \uc2 For many, it's been a slow, ongoing effort. \uc2 UPS\uc2  created an Immersive Technology division in late 2019. At the time, a lot of training was tethered to a physical computer and involved watching a screen, said Mark Grob, who has led the division since its inception.\par \par \uc2 Today, use of VR for some aspects of driver training at \uc2 UPS\uc2  is a mature practice, Grob said. \uc2 UPS\uc2  offers "pre-trip" training in VR, walking trainees through a checklist of tasks they need to complete before hitting the road. It is working to scale beyond drivers to other roles in the business, like package operators at logistics hubs, he said.\par \par \uc2 Police departments are using VR to train officers on how to use their weapons proficiently and how to interact with civilians in the field.\par \par \uc2 "We've had our officers sitting there putting this on their head, and they felt like they were really there, and they're reacting as though they're really on the street. They're yelling at people in the scenario, telling them to drop the weapon," said Chris Botzum, deputy chief of the Joliet Police Department in Joliet, Ill.\par \par \uc2 High-cost, high-risk scenarios like these are where VR training is most valuable, said Nguyen. But companies are also using it for so-called soft skills.\par \par \uc2 London-based financial services company \uc2 St. James's Place\uc2  uses VR to train financial advisers how to engage with clients. In one VR scenario, they meet with mixed-sex couples and then are given feedback based on whether they spend more time addressing the man than the woman -- a common problem in the industry, said Nicki Finnigan, the company's director of learning and development.\par \par \uc2 For skills like this, VR can be more expensive than real-world training, which would typically involve just people in a room speaking, said Finnigan. But she feels it's more effective. Groups of trainees don't have to wait while each one plays out mock scenarios, and they're more comfortable acting and making mistakes in VR than in front of peers.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  uses VR to train store associates in scenarios like how to interact with a frustrated customer. Training in VR also gives the company more data, said Jen Buchanan, vice president of the Walmart Academy training program. The team is able to get better information about which associates made right or wrong choices during the scenario, which helps refine training, she added.\par \par \uc2 Buchanan said \uc2 Walmart\uc2  is leveraging VR for scenarios that are "rare or difficult to practice." When it comes to skills like leadership or teaching company values, gathering associates for an in-person training still makes the most sense, she added. "I think it's based on the scenario."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240909ek990000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
