\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Layoffs Bring Pain to Videogame Workers --- Many who turned their passion into employment suffer through job cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah E. Needleman \par \uc2 1,054 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tarl Raney was a recent college graduate in the early 2000s when he spotted a newspaper ad for what sounded like a dream job.\par \par \uc2 "Want to make money playing videogames?" the listing read. "Hells yea," he thought.\par \par \uc2 Raney worked consistently in the videogame industry for the next two decades, rising from quality-assurance tester to lead producer. His dream job, it seemed, had become a dream career. Then last fall, he was let go along with about 800 others from "Fortnite" maker \uc2 Epic Games\uc2 . He got hired at an indie game studio three months later, only to get laid off again in June.\par \par \uc2 "It was very deflating to happen a second time," said the 47-year-old, who lives in the Dallas area and is back to job hunting. "I love this industry, but I'm worried it's going to happen again."\par \par \uc2 Like filmmaking, sports and music, videogames have long been a competitive business to break into because so many people want to turn their childhood passion into a career. But for those lucky enough to find their place, it has been reliable, as steady industry growth has provided work on one project after another.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, that reliability evaporated. Studios are shutting down, games are being canceled and nearly every week brings news of more layoffs. Thousands like Raney are trying to figure out if they can stick with their passion for videogames or need to find a new line of work.\par \par \uc2 Videogame companies have sacked just over 11,500 employees so far this year, according to an online tally of termination announcements and news reports compiled by Farhan Noor, a technical artist in California. That figure compares with 10,500 in all of 2023 and 8,500 in 2022.\par \par \uc2 As in other tech sectors, some game jobs are being cut because companies went overboard with hiring during the pandemic. But the videogame industry faces unique challenges. More players are sticking with one game for long periods thanks to frequent online updates, rather than finishing one and then moving on to another. Producing and marketing costs are rising because of new technology and new competition for audiences' attention. All these factors have sparked industry consolidation, leading to even more job cuts.\par \par \uc2 "I don't remember this many layoffs in the 24 years I've covered the industry," said \uc2 Wedbush Securities\uc2  analyst Michael Pachter.\par \par \uc2 Some of the most successful companies in videogames, such as "Grand Theft Auto" maker \uc2 Take-Two Interactive Software\uc2 , "Madden NFL" maker \uc2 Electronic Arts\uc2  and "Assassin's Creed" maker \uc2 Ubisoft Entertainment\uc2  have laid off workers over the past year. Many studios have shut down entirely, including ones owned by Xbox maker \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and PlayStation maker \uc2 Sony\uc2 . The employment downturn also has affected videogame-adjacent companies popular with players, like the messaging service \uc2 Discord\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 's streaming platform Twitch.\par \par \uc2 Overall, global consumer spending on games is forecast to grow 3.6% this year after rising 1.8% in 2023 and shrinking 6.3% in 2022, according to analytics firm Aldora Intelligence. The industry grew an unprecedented 23.4% in 2020.\par \par \uc2 "The games industry's focus has shifted from an emphasis on growth to profitability and efficiency," said \uc2 Joost van Dreunen\uc2 , chief executive of Aldora and a professor at \uc2 New York University's Stern School of Business\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Daniel Beahn was laid off from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 -owned \uc2 Bethesda Softworks\uc2  in January 2023 after moving to Baltimore from Austin, Texas, a decade ago for the job, which let him work on some of his favorite franchises like "Fallout" and "Elder Scrolls."\par \par \uc2 During his tenure there, Beahn, 54, moved up the ranks from business-operations manager to lead producer. A highlight, he said, was seeing his name in the credits for several of the company's games.\par \par \uc2 Beahn was dismissed along with about 10,000 other \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  employees. The last time he was handed a pink slip was in 2003, when an \uc2 Electronic Arts\uc2 -owned studio he worked at was closed. He found a new job in a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 This time, it took him nearly 18 months to get his current position, a three-month freelance gig for a startup mobile-game studio.\par \par \uc2 A stepfather of two teenage boys, Beahn said he has blown through his severance package and savings while applying to several hundred jobs. He is now dipping into his 401(k).\par \par \uc2 "I have to retire later or die earlier, neither of which are particularly appealing," he said.\par \par \uc2 The cutthroat job market is especially apparent for game-industry recruiters like Evan Bailey of Newport Beach, Calif., who was let go from \uc2 Activision\uc2  in January, three months after the company was acquired by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and two months before he got married.\par \par \uc2 Bailey, 35, said his layoff was heartbreaking because he was a longtime fan of \uc2 Activision\uc2 's "Call of Duty" franchise and helped form the teams behind four games. The work earned him a black zip-up jacket with patches representing each "Call of Duty" installment he worked on, which he now finds tough to wear. It reminds him of the parties the company held to celebrate game launches, which featured performances by artists like rapper Flo Rida.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody's hiring, so no one's hiring recruiters," said Bailey. "It's just been so brutal."\par \par \uc2 Liana Ruppert got a tattoo on her left arm of a character from the videogame "Destiny" long before she joined the studio that makes it, \uc2 Sony\uc2 -owned Bungie, in 2021. Her job as an online community manager only made her a bigger fan of the shooter series, so when she was let go last October along with about 100 other people, it felt like part of her identity was killed.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Ruppert, 37, hasn't played "Destiny" because it would mean having "to go back to being on the outside looking in," she said. She is juggling freelance gigs and is making enough money to pay her bills, but she lacks job security, health insurance and other benefits.\par \par \uc2 A "Destiny" fan recently recognized her walking around her home city of Seattle and referred to her as Hippy, the nickname she had while working at Bungie.\par \par \uc2 "It made me cry," she said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240909ek9900039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
