\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , Union Agree On 25% Wage Hike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 698 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  agreed to increase wages by 25% for its largest union in a four-year pact aimed at preventing a crippling strike, days before a potential walkout that would further strain the embattled manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 The jet maker, led by new Chief Executive \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , is bleeding cash and falling behind on production as it deals with continued fallout from January's \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  accident in which a door plug blew off a 737 MAX jetliner in midair.\par \par \uc2 The compromise won't necessarily avert a strike when the current contract expires on Thursday. The 33,000 members of the union, who build \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's bestselling 737 MAX and other jets, will vote this week and could still opt to reject it and walk out.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders, who had sought a 40% pay raise over four years, face a membership irate over years of concessions and \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's shift to nonunion labor.\par \par \uc2 Negotiators reached the deal in Sunday morning's early hours. In addition to a 25% pay increase over four years, the deal bolsters retirement benefits, lowers healthcare costs and commits \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to building planes in the unionized Pacific Northwest, a crucial union demand after the company set up a nonunion plant in South Carolina to build the 787 more than a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Union leadership Sunday recommended that members vote in favor of the deal. If members reject the contract and two-thirds vote to strike, workers would walk out at midnight on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Although there was no way to achieve success on every single item, we can honestly say that this proposal is the best contract we've negotiated in our history," Jon Holden, president of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  union chapter, said in a letter to members. "Financially, the company finds itself in a tough position due to many self-inflicted missteps."\par \par \uc2 The union sought to regain benefits lost in previous rounds of talks. Under the deal, average wages would increase 33% over four years because of included seniority increases, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  agreed that its next new airplane would be built in Washington state if the project is launched in the life of the four-year contract, Holden said. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has said it likely won't build a new plane until the next decade. The company also committed to keeping current plane programs, including the 737, in place.\par \par \uc2 "Our team in the Puget Sound region will build \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's next new airplane. Meaning job security for generations to come," \uc2 Boeing\uc2  commercial chief \uc2 Stephanie Pope\uc2  said in a video message to employees.\par \par \uc2 For weeks, members of the union had been staging lunchtime walkouts and in-plant demonstrations to pressure the company leading up to the contract's expiration. In July, 99% of workers voted preliminarily to approve a strike if the two sides failed to reach a deal that members deemed acceptable.\par \par \uc2 The union's members have never rejected a contract recommended by its leaders, though dynamics around this deal are different, said Michel Merluzeau of AIR, an aerospace-industry research company in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 The 25% increase falls short of what the union had said was a hard line, he said. And a membership with more younger workers new to the industry may be less swayed by the new-plane commitment, he said.\par \par \uc2 "How is the leadership going to sell something that in many ways is a little short of what they expected," Merluzeau said.\par \par \uc2 Fallout from even a short strike would cascade through the aerospace industry, further stymying airlines that are short on airplanes necessary to meet resurgent travel demand and stressing the already fragile supply chain. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  burned through $1 billion a month in the first half of 2024, as losses mounted amid sluggish production. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  downgraded \uc2 Boeing\uc2  stock last week on cash concerns.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union have a tumultuous history.\par \par \uc2 Their last labor deal was in 2008. Those negotiations led to a 57-day strike that stymied production, sapping the plane maker's revenue. Three years before that, the union struck for 28 days, protesting rising health premiums and the company's unwillingness to protect jobs from outsourcing.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240909ek990001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
