\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: IT Unemployment Hits 6%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Belle Lin \par \uc2 449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for information-technology workers rose to 6% in August, from 5.6% the prior month, as the boom in artificial intelligence continued to drastically alter the tech landscape.\par \par \uc2 In August, there were 148,000 unemployed IT workers, and more than the 145,000 in July, according to consulting firm Janco Associates, which based its findings on data from the \uc2 U.S. Department of Labor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The IT unemployment rate has been above the national jobless rate for seven of the past eight months, Janco found. On Friday, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  said the national jobless rate ticked down to 4.2% in August as the economy added 142,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Joblessness for IT workers is at its worst since the dot-com bubble burst in the early 2000s, said \uc2 Victor Janulaitis\uc2 , Janco's chief executive. This time the culprit is AI, he said, which is causing "seismic change" comparable to that seen when personal computers came into wide use.\par \par \uc2 Job reductions in IT remain concentrated in traditional technology roles like managing back-end corporate systems, Janulaitis said, while AI and cybersecurity are providing pockets of growth.\par \par \uc2 "The roles that are most in demand right now are roles where candidates have expertise in security, AI and the cloud," said \uc2 Steve Watt\uc2 , chief information officer of \uc2 Hyland Software\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Within AI, businesses are looking for workers who can help them prepare company data for use with AI models, as well as design tools to actually use AI, said \uc2 Vikram Nafde\uc2 , CIO of \uc2 Webster Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Companies in IT services added 3,400 new workers last month, down from 4,000 in July, according to the trade group \uc2 CompTIA\uc2 . The August gains, however, were offset by a loss of 2,500 workers in tech manufacturing -- part of a larger nationwide reduction in factory jobs, \uc2 CompTIA\uc2  said. Tech manufacturing jobs are especially important because chip fabs require a large, specialized workforce.\par \par \uc2 Part of the difficulty recently laid-off IT workers now face is a disconnect between the skills they have and how much they expect to be paid. Big pandemic-era paychecks and IT jobs no longer exist, experts say.\par \par \uc2 For software development and IT support, job postings are about 30% fewer than they were before the pandemic, said \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2 , an economist at jobs site Indeed. High-income sectors like technology also are experiencing slower pay growth, Indeed's wage data shows.\par \par \uc2 "If you're someone looking for a job in that space, there are fewer opportunities than there were pre-Covid, and definitely far fewer opportunities than there were in late 2021 when that labor market was red-hot," Bunker said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich487\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7305
