\par\par \b \uc2 Disney Financial Secrets Revealed --- Leaked data shed light on operations, expose personal information\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert McMillan, Brian Whitton, Robbie Whelan and Caitlin Ostroff \par \uc2 783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Passport numbers for a group of \uc2 Disney\uc2  cruise line workers. \uc2 Disney\uc2 + streaming revenue. Sales of Genie+ theme park passes.\par \par \uc2 The trove of data from \uc2 Disney\uc2  that was leaked online by hackers earlier this summer includes a range of financial and strategy information that sheds light on the entertainment giant's operations, according to files viewed by The Wall Street Journal. It also includes personally identifiable information of some staff and customers.\par \par \uc2 The leaked files include granular details about revenue generated by such products as \uc2 Disney\uc2 + and ESPN+; park pricing offers the company has modeled and what appear to be login credentials for some of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's cloud infrastructure. (The Journal didn't attempt to access any \uc2 Disney\uc2  systems.)\par \par \uc2 "We decline to comment on unverified information The Wall Street Journal has purportedly obtained as a result of a bad actor's illegal activity," a \uc2 Disney\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  told investors in an August regulatory filing that it is investigating the unauthorized release of "over a terabyte of data" from one of its communications systems. It said the incident hadn't had a material impact on its operations or financial performance and doesn't expect that it will.\par \par \uc2 Data that a hacking entity calling itself Nullbulge released online spans more than 44 million messages from \uc2 Disney\uc2 's \uc2 Slack\uc2  workplace communications tool, upward of 18,800 spreadsheets and at least 13,000 PDFs, the Journal found.\par \par \uc2 The scope of the material taken appears to be limited to public and private channels within \uc2 Disney\uc2 's \uc2 Slack\uc2  that one employee had access to. No private messages between executives appear to be included. \uc2 Slack\uc2  is only one online forum in which \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees communicate at work.\par \par \uc2 The exposed financial information was in documents shared by staffers that detail company operations. It isn't official data of the sort \uc2 Disney\uc2  discloses to Wall Street and might not reflect final financial performance for a given period.\par \par \uc2 A spreadsheet exposed in the leak appears to detail revenue generated from Genie+, the premium park pass launched in 2021. The pass is a signature achievement of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's theme-park division chief, and the data underscore how vital Genie+ has become to the financial performance of that unit.\par \par \uc2 The file indicates that the passes generated more than $724 million in pretax revenue between October 2021 and June 2024 at Walt Disney World alone.\par \par \uc2 The leaked documents also provide fresh insight into \uc2 Disney\uc2 's streaming revenue. The company doesn't disclose revenue for individual streaming services within its direct-to-consumer business, which includes \uc2 Disney\uc2 +, \uc2 Hulu\uc2  and ESPN+. Some investors have hungered for more-granular data.\par \par \uc2 Internal spreadsheets suggest that \uc2 Disney\uc2 + generated more than $2.4 billion in revenue in the March quarter. That amounts to about 43% of revenue the company reports for its direct-to-consumer entertainment business, which also includes \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . It underscores how significant a revenue contributor \uc2 Hulu\uc2  is, particularly as \uc2 Disney\uc2  seeks to buy out \uc2 Comcast\uc2 's stake in that streaming service and as the two sides spar over its value.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Slack\uc2  channels in the cache contain detailed information about staff aboard the company's cruises, including passport numbers, visa details, places of birth and physical addresses, as well as some current assignments.\par \par \uc2 Another spreadsheet contained names, addresses and phone numbers for some \uc2 Disney Cruise Line\uc2  passengers. One had names and contact information for a cluster of Disneyland guests with restaurant reservations.\par \par \uc2 Other channels offer glimpses of staff reactions to the company's 2022 battle in Florida over Republican Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's Parental Rights in Education law, which restricts teaching children through grade three about gender identity and sexual orientation. LGBTQ employees and leaders of employee-resource groups wrote letters to then-\uc2 Disney\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2  and other members of staff expressing their frustration that the company hadn't taken a stand. Later the company expressed concern about the law's consequences.\par \par \uc2 In July, Nullbulge uploaded the \uc2 Disney\uc2  data to the internet, where it could be accessed by anyone. Nullbulge couldn't say how many people had downloaded the data because it was uploaded to a decentralized file-sharing network.\par \par \uc2 Nullbulge claims to be a Russia-based hacktivist group that advocates for artist rights, but security researchers believe the hack is the work of a lone individual based in the U.S. In a direct message sent via X in July, Nullbulge said it accessed \uc2 Disney\uc2 's data through a company manager of software development, whose computer they compromised.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Sarah Krouse contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240906ek960000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
