\par\par \b \uc2 Big Brands Pull Back From LGBTQ Index\b0\par\par\uc2 By Theo Francis and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 1,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Ford scaled back its diversity initiatives, it called out one organization by name: the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The automaker told employees last week it would stop providing workplace data to the gay-rights lobbying group, which spent decades persuading big companies to embrace policies hospitable to lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender and queer employees and customers.\par \par \uc2 Other companies dialing down diversity initiatives this summer also said they would distance themselves from \uc2 HRC\uc2 : \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2 , Lowe's, rural retailer \uc2 Tractor Supply\uc2  and distiller \uc2 Brown-Forman\uc2 , which makes Jack Daniel's. On Tuesday, \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  also said it would stop working with the group.\par \par \uc2 The companies didn't elaborate on why they highlighted \uc2 HRC\uc2  in their announcements. Nearly all of them had ranked well on an index \uc2 HRC\uc2  uses to score companies by their LGBTQ-friendly policies. Some of the companies said they would stop sharing data with \uc2 HRC\uc2  after they had been targeted by social-media activist Robby Starbuck.\par \par \uc2 Starbuck said he has been open about his disdain for \uc2 HRC\uc2 's index and policies it supports because he said they could leave conservative customers financing activities with which they disagree, such as transition care for transgender children of employees. He said he doesn't raise the group directly with executives. "I don't think I need to," he said in an interview. "I think the companies put two and two together."\par \par \uc2 The focus on the \uc2 HRC\uc2  -- in particular its equality ranking -- marks a shift in efforts to unwind diversity initiatives in the private sector. This year, companies have mostly faced pressure over workplace and funding programs based on race and ethnicity, including pay incentives, grants and supplier contracting. But a few companies -- including the brewer of Bud Light -- have suffered from backlash over LGBTQ support.\par \par \uc2 In a memo to staff, Ford CEO \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said the company was a "pioneer in providing opportunities to people around the world of all races, genders, and backgrounds." He wrote that Ford had reviewed its policies, taking into account an evolving "external and legal environment related to political and social issues."\par \par \uc2 A Ford spokesman said Farley named \uc2 HRC\uc2  because it is the group employees and others ask about most often. The company has decided to stop aiding all surveys that rank workplaces, including for veterans, people with disabilities and other groups, because of the time and effort required, the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 HRC\uc2 's president, Kelley Robinson, said Ford was "signaling that inclusion and other core values are no longer a priority in the workplace." \uc2 HRC\uc2  said Ford's new stance "will hurt the company's long-term business success, from employee retention to consumer decisions about how they will spend their dollars."\par \par \uc2 Ford, \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  and other companies plan to stop providing data for the Corporate Equality Index produced by \uc2 HRC\uc2 's foundation. Last year, the index scored more than 1,300 companies to help potential customers, employees and others gauge how friendly the company's policies are to LGBTQ people.\par \par \uc2 Those measures include workforce policies prohibiting discrimination for sexual orientation and gender identity, and providing health-insurance and other benefits that treat same-sex and opposite-sex partners the same and cover transition-related care for transgender participants.\par \par \uc2 HRC\uc2  awarded 545 companies a perfect score in the index, including both Ford and \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2 , as well as Lowe's and \uc2 Brown-Forman\uc2 . \uc2 Tractor Supply\uc2  scored 95 out of 100 in the last report, as did tractor maker \uc2 Deere\uc2 . In mid-July, \uc2 Deere\uc2  said it would no longer support "social or cultural awareness parades, festivals or events" but didn't name \uc2 HRC\uc2  or any other groups.\par \par \uc2 HRC\uc2  said it would still evaluate and score companies that don't provide information for the index. The group had begun to penalize companies for recent anti-LGBTQ positions.\par \par \uc2 "These statements obviously undermine our broader work toward LGBTQ+ equality," said Eric Bloem, \uc2 HRC\uc2 's vice president of programs and corporate advocacy. "We have a responsibility to make sure our community has the information that they need, so they understand where they can go to work and feel included, and where they can be using their dollars."\par \par \uc2 HRC\uc2  has begun calling attention to companies distancing themselves from its goals. Visitors to its website in recent days encountered an invitation to send a protest message to companies. "This isn't just policy. It's personal," the group said, alongside logos for Ford and others.\par \par \uc2 Ford, Lowe's and \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  hadn't been publicly criticized by Starbuck when they announced their changes. \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  distanced itself from \uc2 HRC\uc2  a week after Starbuck reached out directly to company executives, threatening "to expose their woke policies," Starbuck said in a post on X. Starbuck sent Lowe's a similar message.\par \par \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  told employees in a memo on Tuesday that halting participation in \uc2 HRC\uc2 's index and other rankings won't affect its employee benefits or reduce its commitment to a culture "where every one of our employees knows they are welcome at our bar."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Brown-Forman\uc2  spokeswoman said the distiller's business and the legal landscape had changed since it launched its diversity-and-inclusion strategy in 2019. A Lowe's spokesman said the retailer strives to create a welcoming environment.\par \par \uc2 Criticizing companies by name is unusual for \uc2 HRC\uc2 , said \uc2 Bob Witeck\uc2 , a communications consultant on LGBTQ issues who helped \uc2 HRC\uc2  with early versions of its corporate index.\par \par \uc2 In 2005, Ford stopped advertising in gay publications after boycott threats from a conservative group. By 2012, the automaker was joining GM and Chrysler, along with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , to co-sponsor Motor City Pride, Detroit's annual LGBTQ pride celebration.\par \par \uc2 "I was always proud of the \uc2 HRC\uc2  rating earned by Ford," said Amanda Redwine, a former Ford employee active in its group for LGBTQ employees. "Those changes to company policy were hard won by real employees who fought their way all the way up to get in front of decision makers and to enlist them in the fight."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Maloney contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240906ek9600040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
