\par\par \b \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Pulls Staff, Cautions On Travel To Brazil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Becky Peterson and Micah Maidenberg \par \uc2 837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  has moved to evacuate employees from Brazil and warned others against traveling to the country, a sign of how \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's ongoing battle against Brazil's highest court over his social-media company X is spilling out to affect some of his other businesses.\par \par \uc2 In an email late last week, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  President \uc2 Gwynne Shotwell\uc2  advised employees not to travel to Brazil for work or pleasure, according to people familiar with the email. The space company also last week took steps to relocate from the country its small group of non-Brazilian employees there, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 For months, Musk has been battling with Brazilian Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Alexandre de Moraes\uc2 , who ordered X to remove a number of accounts that the court said are spreading hate speech and misinformation on the social-media platform. In August, X closed operations in Brazil, citing the need to protect the safety of its staff.\par \par \uc2 The court then last week banned X, saying it would only lift the block when the platform complies with legal requirements, such as paying its fines and appointing a legal director in the country.\par \par \uc2 The court also took action against \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's Starlink, ordering its finances in the country frozen and preventing it from conducting any further financial transactions, the satellite provider said Thursday. The order was based on the court's determination that Starlink, a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  business that uses satellites to provide high-speed internet access, should be held responsible for fines levied against X, according to Starlink.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors putting money into Brazil have recoiled at the move. \uc2 Arthur Lira\uc2 , the powerful leader of Brazil's lower house of Congress, warned over the weekend that the decision threatened to heighten legal uncertainties in the country and scare off other businesses.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Starlink said in a post on X that it was doing everything it could to keep Brazilian customers connected. It said it began legal proceedings before the Brazilian Supreme Court, calling the order against it illegal. At the same time, Starlink said it would comply with a ruling requiring it to block access to X.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Starlink's entanglement in a dispute originally about X is a stark illustration of how some government officials around the world may draw few distinctions between enterprises that Musk runs. Starlink could be especially affected by this because the satellite-communications business must secure approval to offer internet service from regulators in each country where it wants to operate around the world.\par \par \uc2 As of the end of last month, Starlink had won those rights from 105 different countries, according to one of its recent posts on X.\par \par \uc2 Starlink has grown quickly in recent years in Brazil, with the satellite business reporting around 250,000 customers across the South American country. The business has gained ground in the country's agricultural heartland and in the Amazon rainforest, regions where traditional internet service may be unavailable or spotty.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is an important market for Starlink, given its large population and remote areas underserved by terrestrial telecom infrastructure, analysts said. Executives at \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  have said Starlink is best suited serving customers in areas outside of dense cities.\par \par \uc2 "When you talk about squeezing usage and squeezing revenue out of the Southern Hemisphere, there's two countries that stand out: Brazil and Australia," said Chris Quilty, president of consulting firm Quilty Space. For any satellite network like Starlink, "Brazil is one of the crown jewels."\par \par \uc2 Starlink currently charges consumers around $33 a month for service in the country, and around $177 in upfront costs for hardware, according to its website.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  also is pursuing deals to connect businesses with Starlink in Brazil. Earlier this year, agriculture-equipment manufacturer \uc2 Deere\uc2  struck an agreement with Starlink to connect its machines in the field with that service expected to debut this year in parts of the U.S. and Brazil. It couldn't be determined if the Brazilian court's ruling against Starlink would affect those plans. \uc2 Deere\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear what impact, if any, the events have had on Musk's electric-vehicle company \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which doesn't sell its cars in Brazil. Musk met with the former president of Brazil \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  in 2020. Before the meeting, Bolsonaro said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that he would try to convince Musk to bring \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to Brazil, though the plans never came to fruition.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Bolsonaro government made deeper inroads with \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , the space technology company led by Musk. In late 2021, Fabio Faria, then serving as communications minister in Bolsonaro's government, said in a video with Musk posted to X that he was excited about beginning a partnership with Starlink and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Faria has said he personally fast-tracked regulatory approval Starlink needed to enter the Brazilian market, the Journal has reported.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Samantha Pearson contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240905ek950003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
