\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Lyft Streamlines Scooters, Bikes Unit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabela Ojea \par \uc2 439 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said it is further reducing its workforce in a new wave of layoffs as it eliminates standalone dockless bikes and scooters from the business.\par \par \uc2 The ride-hailing company on Wednesday said the move will reduce its operating costs as it focuses resources on its core business. \uc2 Lyft\uc2  will cut 1% of its total employees and expects charges between $34 million and $46 million tied to the layoffs. Most of the charges will be booked in the third quarter, with the remainder in the following quarter.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said there are no changes in its largest markets including New York, Chicago and San Francisco, where it doesn't operate dockless bikes and scooters. The company said it discontinuing dockless scooters in Washington, D.C., and is exploring alternatives in Denver.\par \par \uc2 "We're simply deprioritizing dockless scooters going forward. Riders love our bikes and scooters and we've always expected this part of the business to continue to be a meaningful part of \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's offering now and into the future," the company said.\par \par \uc2 It is the latest round of job cuts under \uc2 Lyft\uc2  Chief Executive David Risher, who has aimed to turn the company around and take more market share from rival \uc2 Uber Technologies\uc2 . \uc2 Lyft\uc2  has been able to gain market share in part from the money it's saving from shrinking its payroll.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts are expected to boost its adjusted earnings by $20 million by the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after the company posted its first quarterly profit and reported record-high scooter and bike rides in large markets such as New York City, but executives have warned of the seasonality of the business and costs to maintain the bikes compared with car rides, as it doesn't own the cars used by its drivers.\par \par \uc2 In a call with analysts in August, Finance Chief Erin Brewer said the third quarter is a big season for bike rides and that \uc2 Lyft\uc2  expects a slight increase in the business in the fourth quarter, but factors such as weather could weigh on demand.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  last year said it was exploring strategic options for its bikes division. The company told The Wall Street Journal at the time that it was looking to sell its fleet or forge a partnership with an investor that infuses cash into the division.\par \par \uc2 The company bought a bike-rental operation in 2018, and the bikes have been popular in several U.S. cities like New York City, where it owns Citi Bike. The company had 2,945 office employees at the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240905ek950000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
