\par\par \b \uc2 Main Street: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's McJob\b0\par\par\uc2 By William McGurn \par \uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and this columnist have little in common. But we do share one thing: We both had summer jobs at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  in college.\par \par \uc2 It's not that surprising, considering how many other people can say the same. According to \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, 1 in 8 Americans have worked at one of its restaurants. At the Democratic convention in Chicago, we further learned that Ms. Harris's husband, Doug Emhoff, also worked under the Golden Arches, where he was once Employee of the Month.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris features her stint at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  in a new campaign ad. It's meant, CNN says, "to solidify her image as the middle-class candidate" and "make former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican ticket appear elitist and out of touch."\par \par \uc2 But some question the vice president's claim. The Washington Free Beacon reports that she never publicly mentioned her stint at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's until she ran for president in 2019, that the job also went unmentioned in her two books and various job applications, that there's been no confirmation from \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  itself. Even the online fact-checker Snopes says it's waiting for further evidence before it can confirm or debunk.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump isn't waiting. On Friday he accused his Democratic rival of making the whole thing up.\par \par \uc2 Now, if Ms. Harris is lying that's certainly fair game. But the Trump campaign may be overlooking a more telling Harris vulnerability here. That would be the glaring absence of successful businesses such as \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's from the "opportunity economy" the vice president says she envisions for America.\par \par \uc2 Given the 12.5% of Americans who got their start at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , that suggests a staggering lack of appreciation for this company's extraordinary reach. And that reach extends well beyond Ms. Harris and Mr. Emhoff. Onetime McDonald's burger flippers include Republican former House Speaker Paul Ryan, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  chief of staff \uc2 Andrew Card\uc2 , actress \uc2 Sharon Stone\uc2  and "Hamilton" creator and star Lin-Manuel Miranda. \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's rightly brags about the benefits it offers employees, such as a tuition-assistance plan that can help them earn a high-school diploma, finish college or even learn English.\par \par \uc2 Still, the most important contribution by \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  is the business itself. It brings jobs to communities, supports the other businesses it buys and sells from, not to mention offers parents of modest means a way to treat the family to a fun meal out.\par \par \uc2 Those who look down on fast-food work miss the point. The real skills McDonald's imparts aren't how to cook a Big Mac or operate the cash register. It's an education in the work culture: teaching employees courtesy toward customers, attentiveness to what your boss needs, cleanliness, cooperation with co-workers -- and the satisfaction of an earned paycheck.\par \par \uc2 These are all soft skills. But they are the skills an employer values, especially in an entry-level worker. For workers with no skills or education, such work is the ladder to opportunity and success. But it starts with getting on that first rung, which businesses such as \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  have provided for millions.\par \par \uc2 But does Ms. Harris have a kind word for what McDonald's and other companies do? Or show any appreciation that the best thing for workers -- and their wages -- is a growing economy that drives employers to compete for their workers' labor?\par \par \uc2 In 2019, when Ms. Harris mentioned her McDonald's job, it was in the context of criticizing the company for not providing a living wage. "If we want to talk about these golden arches being a symbol of the best of America, well, the arches are falling short," she said.\par \par \uc2 Leave aside that most minimum-wage jobs aren't held by a sole wage-earner raising a family of four. The inflation of the Biden-Harris years has put the squeeze on companies such as \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's offering low-cost food -- as well as on their customers. \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's President \uc2 Joe Erlinger\uc2  says a Big Mac that sold for $4.39 in 2019 now costs $5.29 -- a 21% increase.\par \par \uc2 Ditto for minimum-wage hikes. Economists note that the real effect of imposing a minimum-wage law is to price some workers, especially those at the bottom, out of the market. Already McDonald's and other fast-food chains are looking to cut costs by automating jobs. That means fewer opportunities for future Kamala Harrises.\par \par \uc2 There's also the PRO Act. At a Labor Day rally Monday, Ms. Harris promised to pass it if elected. Of its many dubious parts surely among the worst is that it would make a \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's restaurant worker a joint employee of the corporation. That would destroy the franchise model in which each \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's is run by its local owner -- and eliminate one popular path to small-business ownership for workers.\par \par \uc2 If elected in November, Ms. Harris and her husband will be the first McDonald's couple in the White House -- a powerful rebuke to those who dismiss flipping burgers as a dead-end job. If she truly believes in the Opportunity Economy, maybe her message to all those with McJobs should be, "You too can be president."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Write to mcgurn@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Say More About \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 " -- WSJ Sept. 14, 2024)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240903ek930003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s