\par\par \b \uc2 World News: Gaza Hostage Killings Spark Strikes --- Political divide deepens as funeral of American-Israeli draws large crowds\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dov Lieber in Tel Aviv and Anat Peled in Jerusalem \par \uc2 677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Israel's political divide deepened Monday, as cities were hit by a national strike and people took to the streets to protest the government's failure to reach a deal to free hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tensions jumped after authorities said over the weekend that the Palestinian militant group had killed six hostages in captivity. Many Israelis said they felt Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  and other leaders had in effect condemned them to certain death. Tens of thousands of Israelis marched in the streets Sunday night and protests continued on Monday night, as four of the six dead hostages were laid to rest.\par \par \uc2 Bezalel Smotrich\uc2 , Israel's hard-right finance minister, criticized union leaders for striking, saying the head of Israel's largest labor union, Arnon Bar-David, was "fulfilling the dream of [\uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader Yahya] Sinwar."\par \par \uc2 After saying for days that Israel had killed the hostages with its bombing campaign, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  issued a new statement Monday suggesting it had executed them and would kill more hostages if Israel's military attempted further rescue missions.\par \par \uc2 "Military pressure equals death and failure. An exchange deal equals freedom and life," the group said in a statement written in Hebrew over an image of a militant holding a handgun before a captive Israeli soldier sitting with his head bowed in a tunnel.\par \par \uc2 The developments showed how the outburst of fury over the fate of the hostages seems to be hardening the positions that have deeply divided Israel even as its troops are fighting on multiple fronts.\par \par \uc2 Netanyahu's coalition of right-wing, ultranationalist and religious political parties has mostly supported his hard-line stance in negotiations with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The prime minister's ultranationalist partners have also threatened to topple the government if a hostage deal comes at the price of ending the war.\par \par \uc2 Israel's political divisions predate the war. Before Oct. 7, Israelis protested for months the Netanyahu government's plan for sweeping changes to the judicial system. The hostages' deaths have raised concerns that Israel could once again become consumed with infighting amid a devastating war with no clear end in sight.\par \par \uc2 The latest protests "will revive the rift within Israeli society," Abraham Diskin, a senior fellow with the Jerusalem-based think tank Kohelet Policy Forum. "Massive protests won't get Netanyahu to change his mind."\par \par \uc2 A poll released Monday by the Jerusalem-based Jewish People Policy Institute found that 49% of the Jewish Israelis it surveyed supported Netanyahu's position that Israeli troops shouldn't leave the Gaza-Egypt border, even if that meant there wouldn't be a deal to free the remaining hostages, while 43% thought troops should leave the corridor to facilitate a deal.\par \par \uc2 Netanyahu and his supporters argue Israel needs to control the border to keep \uc2 Hamas\uc2  from smuggling weapons into Gaza and rearming after the war. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is demanding that Israel leave the area known as the Philadelphi Corridor, for a deal to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Israel's military has said it can secure the border without a permanent presence as long as it can conduct raids when necessary. Netanyahu said Monday that he doesn't believe the international community would allow Israel to go back in any way once it leaves.\par \par \uc2 The Netanyahu government's failure to strike a cease-fire deal and bring an end to the fighting in Gaza has frustrated Western allies of Israel. President Biden was asked Monday if Netanyahu was doing enough to get a hostage deal. "No," he said, as he returned to the White House from a beach vacation.\par \par \uc2 The nationwide strike affected schools, government offices, public transportation, hospitals, banks and Ben Gurion International Airport, the country's main airport.\par \par \uc2 A small group of right-wing counterprotesters gathered outside the prime minister's office in Jerusalem to push for an increase in military pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  inside Gaza in response to the killing of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240903ek930000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
