\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Hotel Workers Strike On Holiday Weekend\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ginger Adams Otis \par \uc2 333 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of hotel workers at properties across the U.S. went on strike over Labor Day weekend after months of contract talks with their employers failed to produce an agreement.\par \par \uc2 The strike, scheduled to last for two or three days, came as the holiday weekend was in full swing. Roughly 10,000 housekeepers, front-desk clerks, kitchen and restaurant staffers, bartenders, doormen and bellhops walked off the job Sunday at properties from Hawaii to Boston.\par \par \uc2 Strikes were in effect at hotels in Honolulu and Kauai, Hawaii; Seattle; San Diego, San Jose and San Francisco, Calif.; Greenwich, Conn.; and Boston on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The workers are employed at mainly \uc2 Hilton\uc2 , \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  and \uc2 Marriott\uc2  properties. The hotels have been engaged in contract talks with the workers union, \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , since May.\par \par \uc2 Hilton remains committed to negotiating for "fair and reasonable agreements," a spokesperson said Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  said it had contingency plans in place to minimize guest disruptions during the labor action. The company has a history of cooperation with its employee unions, including \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 "We are disappointed that \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  has chosen to strike while \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  remains willing to negotiate," said Michael D'Angelo, Hyatt America's head of labor relations.\par \par \uc2 Marriott\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , which represents a range of jobs in the hospitality industry, said its members have been overworked and underpaid since the pandemic forced staffing and service cuts that were never restored. The union is pushing for higher wages and more jobs. "We're on strike because the hotel industry has gotten off track," said Gwen Mills, international president of \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 . "We won't accept a 'new normal' where hotel companies profit by cutting their offerings to guests and abandoning their commitments to workers."\par \par \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  members won contracts last year after a series of strikes at Los Angeles hotels and a 47-day strike at Detroit casinos.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240903ek930000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
