\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Threat of a Strike by Dockworkers This Fall Hangs Over U.S. Seaports\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Efforts to avoid a walkout by dockworkers at some of the nation's busiest seaports are entering a crucial phase.\par \par \uc2 Union officials representing 45,000 workers at ports from Maine to Texas will meet for two days in New Jersey starting Wednesday to discuss wage demands and prepare to strike on Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 Harold Daggett, the head of the union that represents dockworkers at U.S. East and Gulf Coast ports, said recently the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  and employers are "at an impasse" over wages and that his members will walk out if the union doesn't reach a new labor deal before the current multiyear contract expires on Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 The union and the United States Maritime Alliance, which represents ocean shipping companies and port employers, have been talking informally for months, but still haven't begun formal talks that will include thorny issues such as the use of automated machinery on the docks.\par \par \uc2 The ILA is pushing for a 77% increase for workers over six years, according to a person familiar with the talks, far more than the 32% increase the \uc2 International Longshore and Warehouse Union\uc2  won for dockworkers at West Coast ports last year.\par \par \uc2 The person said employers have offered about half that, but the ILA is holding out for more. A spokesman for the union declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Vincent Clerc\uc2 , head of Danish carrier \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2 , said on an earnings call in August that an East Coast strike "would be a really big bottleneck in a very traveled trading route," but that he didn't believe a strike was likely.\par \par \uc2 Other shipping industry officials as well as business groups representing major importers are more concerned. \uc2 Rolf Habben Jansen\uc2 , CEO of German shipping line \uc2 Hapag-Lloyd\uc2 , said on a recent earnings call that the risk of a strike "has really gone up."\par \par \uc2 An ILA walkout would hit major ports at New Jersey, Virginia, Savannah, Ga., and Houston at the busiest time of year for retailers bringing in holiday-season goods.\par \par \uc2 A walkout would also come just a few weeks before the presidential election, presenting a challenge to the Biden administration as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  faces former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Groups such as \uc2 the Retail Industry Leaders Association\uc2 , which represents importers like Target, \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , are calling on the Biden administration to intervene to broker a deal.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Acting Labor Secretary Julie Su helped shepherd an agreement between West Coast dockworkers and employers. Daggett, however, has said he doesn't want the administration "to interfere in our negotiations." The union has filed paperwork with the \uc2 Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service\uc2  that effectively clears the way for workers to walk out on Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the Labor Department said officials are monitoring the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Fears of a strike have prompted importers this year to pull forward orders for goods ahead of the fall retail shopping season, causing an earlier-than-usual flood of cargo, and pushing up shipping rates. "The prospect of an East Coast shutdown comes up in every client meeting," said Nathan Strang, director of ocean freight at freight forwarder \uc2 Flexport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The early surge of cargo is exacerbating strains on ocean shipping networks caused by attacks by Yemen's Houthi rebels on commercial vessels in the Red Sea. Diversions of containerships away from the \uc2 Suez Canal\uc2  that opens into the Red Sea are adding 10 days or more to sailing times from Asia to the U.S. and Europe, eating up cargo capacity as ships take longer routes around the Horn of Africa.\par \par \uc2 Almost 1.4 million containers, measured in 20-foot equivalent units, were shipped from Asia to North America in June, a record for that month, according to transportation data firm Xeneta. The average short-term contract rate to ship a 40-foot container from Asia to the U.S. East Coast at the end of August was $9,518, the firm said, more than double the cost in April.\par \par \uc2 Port tensions are mounting as dockworkers from Germany to Australia have slowed and stopped operations in disputes over pay and working conditions. Last year, U.S. West Coast dockworkers sporadically disrupted cargo flows from Washington state to California during tense talks.\par \par \uc2 Unionized dockworkers in the U.S. enjoy some of the nation's best-paying, blue-collar jobs. The hourly base pay for an ILA dockworker at the start of 2022 was $38, according to collective bargaining agreements which also show the base pay for a West Coast ILWU dockworker in January that year was $46.\par \par \uc2 Because of work rules and overtime opportunities, unionized workers such as crane operators on both coasts can earn annual salaries totaling hundreds of thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Denmark-based marine consulting firm Sea-Intelligence warned that it would take ports a long time to untangle the cargo pileup of even a brief strike. It would take about five days to clear cargo after just a one-day stoppage, the firm estimated, while a one-week strike during the first week of October wouldn't clear until about mid-November.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240831ek8v0002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
