\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Goldman to Lay Off Over 1,300 Workers --- The job cuts are part of an annual review process that culls low performers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Thomas \par \uc2 549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  plans to eliminate more than 1,300 employees from its global workforce as part of an annual review process to cull the low performers, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The bank will likely lay off between 3% and 4% of its workforce, the people said. That would affect between 1,300 and 1,800 people, roughly, given that Goldman employed about 45,300 people as of late last year. The cuts are expected across the bank's various divisions, with some teams impacted more than others.\par \par \uc2 Typically, Goldman aims to trim between 2% and 7% of its workforce annually based on various performance factors. That range has fluctuated over the years based on market conditions and Goldman's financial outlook. Layoffs have already started and will continue through the fall, one of the people said. They are part of an annual review process known at Goldman as a "strategic resource assessment," or SRA.\par \par \uc2 "Our annual talent reviews are normal, standard, and customary, but otherwise unremarkable," said Tony Fratto, a Goldman spokesman. Overall head count at Goldman is expected to be higher at the end of 2024 compared to 2023, he noted.\par \par \uc2 The annual culling process measures performance using a number of variables. One factor that has become more relevant is in-office attendance. During the pandemic and its aftermath, Goldman and its peers relaxed requirements for employees to work out of firm offices. But banks are beginning to crack down on employees who don't regularly appear in person.\par \par \uc2 Goldman and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  are among the banks that expect many employees to work from an office most days.\par \par \uc2 Other major banks, including \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , have similar initiatives to cull underperformers each year.\par \par \uc2 Goldman had paused its SRA program during the pandemic -- when dealmaking hit record levels -- but picked it back up in 2022, when the cuts were at the lower end of its historical range. That year, Goldman cut a few hundred employees in September. It then eliminated roughly 3,200 positions, or about 6% of employees, in January 2023, and prepped more layoffs that May as dealmaking slumped. Last fall, as part of its annual SRA program, Goldman made more cuts.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, a more stable outlook for interest rates has been giving workers across Wall Street hope that dealmaking is emerging from a yearslong drought. Still, an economic setback or political upheaval could derail activity at any moment. The looming U.S. presidential election is also giving some dealmakers pause.\par \par \uc2 Goldman, for its part, has refocused on its Wall Street operations after deciding to exit from consumer lending.\par \par \uc2 In July, Goldman posted a 21% increase in investment-banking revenue in the second quarter compared with a year ago. The bank also booked a 27% revenue jump in its asset- and wealth-management business.\par \par \uc2 "From what we're seeing, we are in the early innings of a capital markets and M&A recovery," Goldman Chief Executive \uc2 David Solomon\uc2  said on a call with analysts in mid-July.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs at Goldman come after an abysmal bonus season for workers who worked on fewer deals in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240831ek8v0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
