\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Fresh Data Give Boost To a 'Soft Landing'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Corrie Driebusch and Will Parker \par \uc2 254 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy grew more than previously thought in the second period, new data show, bolstering the case that the country may be experiencing a so-called soft landing.\par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product grew at a 3% seasonally and inflation-adjusted annual rate, the Commerce Department said in its revised estimate.\par \par \uc2 That is up from the 2.8% rate reported last month, and it is far above the first quarter's weak 1.4% expansion.\par \par \uc2 Initial jobless claims for the week ended Aug. 24 also fell slightly, according to the Labor Department, another positive sign for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Despite the economy's strength, Federal Reserve policymakers appear all but certain to begin cutting rates when they next meet in September. Recent data show that inflation continues to cool, while the labor market shows some signs of weakness.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a housing-market recovery is still a ways off, even as lower mortgage rates and higher inventory have improved conditions for potential buyers. Pending home sales fell 5.5% in July from the month prior and fell 8.5% from July of last year, the \uc2 National Association of Realtors\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Home prices have continued to climb. The median existing-home price rose 4.2% in July from a year ago, \uc2 NAR\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 One sign of relief for prospective buyers: Mortgage rates fell to a fresh 15-month low, a survey of lenders released by \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
