\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- \uc2 Teamsters Union\uc2  Tries to Thwart Canada Order Calling for Quick End to Freight-Rail Stoppage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 652 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- Canada's attempt to restart freight-rail traffic amid a labor conflict ran into hurdles Friday, with the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  both contesting the government's authority to impose binding arbitration and serving a fresh strike notice at one of the country's two main railroads.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Labor Minister Steven MacKinnon sought Thursday to bring an end to a labor stoppage at both \uc2 Canadian National Railway\uc2  and Canadian Pacific Kansas City, arguing the two sides were at an impasse and the likelihood of a success at the bargaining table was grim. On Thursday, just after midnight, the railroads locked out over 9,000 employees represented by the Teamsters Canada Rail Conference, halting freight-rail traffic across Canada and the U.S.-Canada border, and disrupting North America's supply-chain networks.\par \par \uc2 Economists at \uc2 Moody's\uc2  and \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  have warned a labor shutdown could cost the Canadian economy hundreds of millions of dollars a day. Business leaders in both Canada and the U.S. pressed the Liberal government in Ottawa to intervene, warning a prolonged shutdown on rail traffic in Canada could bring severe repercussions for North America.\par \par \uc2 Business executives have warned that U.S. agricultural exports could fail to reach foreign markets, clothing and accessories wouldn't make it to retail shelves during the crucial back-to-school shopping season, and popular vehicle models wouldn't reach dealerships, business groups said.\par \par \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  moved on two fronts Friday that risk thwarting the government's plan.\par \par \uc2 The union is formally contesting the government's authority to impose final binding arbitration on \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  talks with CPKC. The \uc2 Canada Industrial Relations Board\uc2  is hearing arguments Friday. A spokesman for the board declined to provide a timeline on when the independent, quasi-judicial body might rule.\par \par \uc2 The labor board must decide whether it will enforce the labor minister's directive. A \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  official said the union is prepared to go to the Federal Court of Appeal should the labor board side with the government.\par \par \uc2 Calgary, Alberta-based CPKC said the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' filing before the labor board will delay its attempt to resume operations. "CPKC remains prepared to resume service as soon as it is ordered to do so by the" labor board, it said. \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  represents more than 3,000 workers at CPKC.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  served Canadian National with a strike notice, just hours after the union had said members would be returning to work at the Montreal-based railroad. The union members would walk off the job on Monday at 10 a.m. Eastern time unless a new labor deal is reached.\par \par \uc2 "We do not believe that any of the matters we have been discussing over the last several days are insurmountable and we remain available for discussion to resolve this matter without a further work stoppage," union leadership said in a letter to Canadian National.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Canadian National said operations at the railroad resumed Friday morning. "While Canadian National is focused on its recovery plan to get back to powering the economy, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are focused on returning to the picket line and holding the country hostage to their demands," the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Canadian Labor Minister MacKinnon declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian railroads gradually wound down their operations in the past two weeks in preparation for a possible labor disruption. U.S. railroads that move loads on Canadian networks said they were forced to adjust their operations.\par \par \uc2 Canadian National operates about 20,000 miles of tracks, connecting Canada's east and west coasts with the U.S. South, and carries about 300 million metric tons of cargo annually. CPKC's network also runs about 20,000 miles, serving markets across Canada, the U.S. and Mexico, and in 2023 transported roughly 350 million metric tons of cargo.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240824ek8o00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
