\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Orders Railroads to Cease Lockout After Freight Is Disrupted\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira and Esther Fung \par \uc2 671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- Canada ordered an end Thursday to a work stoppage at the country's two main railroads, saying an arbitrator would resolve disagreements between the companies and a union that disrupted freight traffic and supply chains across North America.\par \par \uc2 "It is the government's responsibility to ensure industrial peace," said Steven MacKinnon, Canada's labor minister. He said negotiations between the two railroads -- \uc2 Canadian National Railway\uc2  and Canadian Pacific Kansas City -- and the Teamsters Canada Rail Conference had reached an impasse.\par \par \uc2 He said he expected Canadian National and CPKC to resume operations within days.\par \par \uc2 The decision brings a rapid end to a countrywide labor disruption that halted freight-rail traffic used to transport grains, energy products, chemicals, fertilizer and other durable goods across Canada and into the U.S. Canadian National and CPKC locked out over 9,000 employees represented by \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  just after midnight on Thursday, after failing to clinch deals ahead of a deadline.\par \par \uc2 "We gave negotiations every possible opportunity to succeed. We see very little prospect of that. The parties are very, very far apart," MacKinnon said at a press conference. Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  had earlier signaled the government was prepared to bring the labor conflict to an end.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders in both Canada and the U.S. pressed the Liberal government to intervene, warning that a prolonged shutdown on rail traffic in Canada could bring severe repercussions for North America.\par \par \uc2 Business groups said U.S. agricultural exports could fail to reach foreign markets, clothing and accessories wouldn't make it to retail shelves during the crucial back-to-school shopping season, and popular vehicle models wouldn't reach dealerships.\par \par \uc2 MacKinnon said he has asked the \uc2 Canada Industrial Relations Board\uc2  to impose binding arbitration on the talks.\par \par \uc2 Montreal-based Canadian National said it had ended its lockout, and would implement a recovery plan while awaiting formal orders from the industrial-relations board. CPKC, based in Calgary, Alberta, said it was preparing to restart railway operations, with further details on timing once it hears from the labor board. "We regret that the government had to intervene," said CPKC Chief Executive \uc2 Keith Creel\uc2 . "However, given the stakes for all involved this situation required action."\par \par \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  President Paul Boucher said the union would consult with its lawyers on next steps. Boucher said the railroads "manufactured this crisis . . . and manipulated the government to once again disregard the rights afforded to working-class Canadians."\par \par \uc2 A seven-day work stoppage at the two railroads could have cost the Canadian and U.S. economies over $1 billion, with most of the pain in Canada, according to estimates from Anderson Economic Group, a consulting firm that specializes in loss assessments from strikes and global conflicts. Beyond that, "the impact in both the U.S. and Canada would be much worse," with agriculture, manufacturing and energy sustaining acute strains, the firm said.\par \par \uc2 Union Pacific Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Vena\uc2  said in a letter this week to MacKinnon that the stoppage halts more than 2,500 railcars that cross the U.S.-Canada border daily in the company's rail network.\par \par \uc2 "A prolonged shutdown will have devastating consequences on the North American economy," said Vena. "Along with supply chain delays and shortages, mines, grain elevators, factories, mills and other industries will incur increased costs when they lose their rail service."\par \par \uc2 The two Canadian railroads transported the equivalent of about $280 billion of goods last year, with both companies bringing cargo arriving at coastal ports in Canada across the country and into the U.S. The \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Transportation Statistics\uc2  estimates that, in 2023, rail accounted for about 16%, or $114 billion, of the freight that moved between the U.S. and Canada.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian railroads gradually wound down their operations in the past 10 days or so in preparation for a possible disruption. U.S. railroads that move loads on Canadian networks said they were adjusting their operations.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240823ek8n0006b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
