\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Rail Dispute Poses Risk to Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 945 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- Chemical manufacturers and agricultural producers face some of the biggest risks to businesses across Canada from a potential halt to much of the country's rail operations in a labor dispute that threatens to stanch cross-border trade and upend North American supply chains.\par \par \uc2 Both Canadian Pacific Kansas City and \uc2 Canadian National Railway\uc2  planned to lock out employees represented by the Teamsters Canada Rail Conference starting at 12:01 a.m. Eastern time Thursday unless new agreements are reached.\par \par \uc2 Both the companies and \uc2 Teamsters Canada\uc2  say the sides are at an impasse, with the union saying the railroads are demanding concessions that threaten worker safety. The railroads have denied that. A union official said wages aren't holding up an agreement.\par \par \uc2 Talks continued in Montreal and Calgary, Alberta, between the union and the railroads ahead of the deadline. Late Wednesday, Canadian National called on the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  to intervene and avoid a work stoppage, claiming the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  was thwarting progress. "A deal is not possible without a willing partner," Canadian National said. A union spokesman said the railroad has demanded concessions, and now wants the government "to save them from themselves."\par \par \uc2 Canada's labor minister, Steven MacKinnon, had earlier declined calls by business groups to intervene, saying it is the responsibility of the union and railroads to negotiate a new agreement.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  urged the sides to reach a deal, adding that households, farmers and businesses were counting on a resolution.\par \par \uc2 The threat of a work stoppage was already reaching into Canada's economy this week as the country's two big railroads started to curtail operations in anticipation that trains would stop moving. Experts warned the actions would throttle businesses from retailers to automakers and even reach into commuter trains that use tracks operated by the freight railroads.\par \par \uc2 The Western Grain Elevator Association, whose members -- among them commodities giant Cargill and \uc2 Glencore\uc2 -backed \uc2 Viterra\uc2  -- handle 90% of western Canada's bulk grain exports, warned a labor outage would cost the sector roughly $36 million a day.\par \par \uc2 "When we lose shipping, we don't get it back," said \uc2 Wade Sobkowich\uc2 , the association's executive director. The risk, he said, is that deliveries may be delayed until early 2025, when the Australian harvest emerges and global prices tend to drop. "There's limited capacity to store grain and so if we're not moving grain by rail, we're not bringing it in," Sobkowich said.\par \par \uc2 Both carriers stopped taking in hazardous materials shipments the week before the deadline, citing safety concerns about having potentially dangerous goods idled in their networks. That had an immediate impact on the country's big chemical producers.\par \par \uc2 Bob Masterson\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Chemistry Industry Association of Canada\uc2 , said stockpiles of chlorine, which Canadian municipalities used in water-treatment plants, are "now depleting." Canada is the world's biggest exporter of chlorine, which kills bacteria and is used in consumer products such as bleach, paints and drugs.\par \par \uc2 "If this isn't addressed and the chlorine cannot flow again, you are talking about boil-water advisories to protect public health," said Masterson, whose members ship over $20 billion of goods annually on Canada's main railroads.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Chemistry Council\uc2  also raised alarms. The U.S. imports nearly $25 billion of chemicals from Canada, the council said. The Washington-based trade group said Canada supplies about 60% of the chlorine used in water-treatment plants in western U.S. states.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Sloan, the council's senior director of regulatory affairs, said U.S. chemical producers could throttle production due to the labor stoppage, given the integration between the U.S. and Canadian economies.\par \par \uc2 "You will have to start slowing down production pretty quickly if railcars aren't being picked up or delivered," Sloan said.\par \par \uc2 Montreal-based CN operates about 20,000 miles of tracks, connecting Canada's east and west coasts with the U.S. South, and carries about 300 million metric tons of cargo annually. CPKC's network also runs about 20,000 miles, serving markets across Canada, the U.S. and Mexico, and in 2023 transported roughly 350 million metric tons of cargo.\par \par \uc2 The two railroads also own tracks used by passenger rail operations in Toronto, Montreal and Vancouver, British Columbia, that could be curtailed in a labor outage. Roughly 7,500 commuters use one such line daily from the city of Milton outside Toronto as part of the region's GO train services, for instance, said Andrea Ernesaks, a spokeswoman for GO operator \uc2 Metrolinx\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both freight carriers have substantial business in the U.S. and connect to U.S. railroads.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Transportation Statistics\uc2  estimates that in 2023. rail accounted for about 16%, or $114 billion, of the freight that moves annually between the U.S. and Canada.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian Chamber of Commerce said a simultaneous labor stoppage would affect the flow of about $730 million in goods a day, and reduce access to materials needed to build cars and homes, and provide heating for households.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\uc2  warned that imports of Canadian grains and oilseeds "would be heavily impacted" by a simultaneous railroad labor stoppage.\par \par \uc2 Canada's beef and pork producers say they might have to close processing plants should the labor stoppage extend beyond a week.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian Meat Council and Canadian Pork Council noted last summer's two-week strike involving more than 7,000 workers at Canada's Pacific Coast ports affected shipments totaling over $7 billion, and reduced Canada's economic output by $730 million.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Robb M. Stewart contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich474\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7110
