\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Data Show Job Market Was Weaker Than Reported\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Goldfarb \par \uc2 485 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The job market from early 2023 through early this year wasn't as hot as it seemed at the time, new government data suggested Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Compared with the still-official numbers, employers might have added 818,000 fewer jobs in the 12 months through March, the Labor Department said. That means the economy could have added around 178,000 jobs a month over that period, as opposed to the current estimate of 246,000 jobs a month.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's report marked the first step of an annual process by which the Labor Department updates older payroll figures using data from state unemployment-tax records that is more comprehensive, but less timely, than its monthly employer survey. It is only a preliminary estimate of a revision that will be made to the official numbers in February.\par \par \uc2 Investors had already been anticipating a downward adjustment for the period in question. Many haven't quite believed that the labor market was as strong as payroll figures have suggested because other data points -- such as a rising unemployment rate -- have told a less rosy story. (The unemployment rate is based on a separate survey, of households instead of employers.)\par \par \uc2 A surprisingly big increase in the unemployment rate last month has increased speculation that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might cut interest rates by a larger-than-normal half a percentage point at its next meeting on Sept. 17-18 -- although other, more reassuring data have tempered those bets.\par \par \uc2 Central-bank officials from around the world will meet in Wyoming this week, and Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  could provide hints about the Fed's thinking in a speech on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Market reaction to Wednesday's report was muted. Stocks notched modest gains, with the S&P 500 rising 0.4%. The yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note settled at 3.778%, down from 3.818% Tuesday. Yields on shorter-term Treasurys fell more, a sign that traders think the Fed might have to cut rates a little more aggressively than they had previously anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's report doesn't necessarily mean that job gains in the months after March will be revised downward. The payroll growth suggested by the preliminary estimate could also be revised upward when the actual adjustment is made in February. That has been the case in each of the past four years, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysts wrote in a recent report.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  wrote Wednesday that they expect the eventual revision to be about half the size of the initial estimate. Some analysts have also noted that revised payroll figures could be misleading because they might not take into account a recent surge in immigration that has powered the job market.\par \par \uc2 Unauthorized immigrant workers are likely to be included when the government surveys businesses to ask them about their employees.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240822ek8m00063\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
