\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Maersk\uc2  Retracts Message, Accepts Canada Cargo\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  said it is continuing to accept Canada-bound cargoes, saying it had mistakenly relayed inaccurate information to customers on the shipments, as logistics companies and shippers brace for a pending labor disruption this week at Canada's two major railroads.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Maersk\uc2  spokesman said in an email that the Danish shipping line has reviewed contingency plans in the event of a full shutdown, but "we continue to accept bookings to and from Canada, including ocean, rail, truck and air at this time."\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the company had said it was beginning to restrict shipments, a move prompted by the looming possibility of a strike or lockout starting as soon as Thursday, at both Canadian National Railway and Canadian Pacific Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 "Somebody made a mistake," said Lauren Doll, \uc2 Maersk\uc2 's head of communications for the Americas. "There was a miscommunication" among teams in different global regions. "The statement was not accurate and we had it taken down as soon as we could," she said.\par \par \uc2 Over the past week, the Canadian railroads have gradually wound down their operations in preparation for a labor disruption, and are no longer transporting hazardous goods, among other shipments.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian railroads say their negotiations with the Teamsters Canada Rail Conference union appear to be at an impasse.\par \par \uc2 German shipping line \uc2 Hapag-Lloyd\uc2  said in a customer notice that a labor stoppage could disrupt the delivery of goods by rail in Canada and some U.S. regions. The company said it is attempting to work with rail companies to minimize disruptions.\par \par \uc2 ITS Logistics, a third-party logistics company, said some Canada-bound ocean cargo was already shifting to U.S. West Coast ports as carriers look to avoid any rail disruption in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Some goods destined for Canada that move by rail through Chicago will instead shift to trucks, said Paul Brashier, vice president of global supply chain at ITS. "When and if this happens, it will drive rates through the roof overnight, if [trucking] capacity can be obtained at all," he said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. railroads that move loads on Canadian networks said they are adjusting their operations to the embargoes by Canada's railroads.\par \par \uc2 CSX\uc2 , which primarily serves the Northeastern U.S., said it halted certain cross-border shipments connected to Canadian National and CPKC over the weekend. \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  said it shut down all its inbound connections to CPKC intermodal lines on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "Our team is working diligently to minimize any potential impact on your operations and keep our network fluid," \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  said in a customer notice.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Paul Page contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240821ek8l0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
