\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Long Lines, Graffiti Spoil National Parks --- Strain is showing at Zion, Yellowstone and elsewhere with fewer services, reduced staffing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Pohle and Jim Carlton \par \uc2 1,387 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Zion National Park, Utah -- On busy days, one of the most beautiful spots in the U.S. struggles to keep visits enjoyable for its guests. Most days at Zion from March through October are busy.\par \par \uc2 One Monday in July, e-bikers sped down a long, flat trail, nearly buzzing families strolling by. Travelers inched forward in a long line for the park's shuttle bus, then stood shoulder to shoulder once they boarded. As temperatures stretched well into the 90s, hikers had to step on native plants to avoid running into each other along popular trails.\par \par \uc2 Handling throngs of visitors has long been a tough task for a dwindling number of Park Service employees. The task has gotten tougher as visitation has jumped and staffing has shrunk.\par \par \uc2 The amount of full-time equivalent park employees in the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  fell 20% between the 2010 and 2023 fiscal years, according to federal data. Park visitation rose 16% to 325.5 million over that stretch, and the park service got more land to oversee.\par \par \uc2 Officials now face difficult choices about how to manage some of America's most prized parcels of land. In smaller parks, they close visitor centers and bathrooms and stop tours when they don't have staff to manage them. Travelers say they've come to expect reduced services or to spend a good chunk of their day waiting in line at popular parks. Rangers report more vandalism and damage.\par \par \uc2 "The Park Service does not get a budget increase when gas prices go up or utility costs go up," says Jon Jarvis, former director of the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  from 2009 to 2017. "They just have to absorb it."\par \par \uc2 In larger parks such as Zion, funding from nonprofit partners and labor from volunteers helps fill gaps. How best to staff the park remains an exercise in triage, former employees say. Visitation has increased over 70% since 2010 at Zion. It welcomed more than 4.6 million people in 2023. The number of full-time equivalent employees has dropped about 8% in that stretch, according to federal figures.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Austin, 62 years old, waited in the July sun at Zion for a shuttle bus with five family members. They had visited several other national parks during a 17-day road trip. Zion, memorable for its sandstone cliffs and mazes of canyons, was the most jam-packed from its parking lots to its trails.\par \par \uc2 "Given the crowds, I don't think they can change anything to make it better," said Austin, from southwestern Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Visitation to parks accelerated during the Park Service's centennial celebration that began in 2015, then skyrocketed during peak pandemic years.\par \par \uc2 Government funding for staffing hasn't kept up. Republicans and Democrats are at odds over how much more that funding could shrink. The latest funding bill, which passed the House before the summer recess, would eliminate more than 400 positions next year, according to Democrats on Capitol Hill. A spokeswoman for the Republican-led \uc2 House Appropriations Committee\uc2  says a recent one-time influx was enough to restore some past staffing cuts and that the number of personnel assigned to parks isn't directly correlated to budgets.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  manages an appropriated budget of about $3.5 billion when it needs closer to $5 billion for staffing and capital improvements, says Kristen Brengel, senior vice president for government affairs at the \uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think Americans love their parks, but they don't want them loved to death," says Sen. \uc2 Jeff Merkley\uc2 , an Oregon Democrat. "We do not have enough staff."\par \par \uc2 The definitive way the Park Service can manage rising costs is to reduce its personnel budget, says Jarvis, the former National Park's head. This can create a vicious circle: Understaffing wears on morale, leading park workers to rethink their career choices, leading to more departures, he says.\par \par \uc2 Scarce affordable housing prevents many would-be employees from accepting jobs, former Park Service employees say. Because most jobs are seasonal, employees must regularly apply for new jobs and housing.\par \par \uc2 Sue Fritzke, who retired last year as superintendent of Capitol Reef National Park in Utah, says in the five years she worked there, she was able to hire one additional full-time employee, bringing its staff to 29 people. Total annual visitors soared by 67% to 1.5 million over that time.\par \par \uc2 Fritzke says she closed the visitor center at 4:30 p.m. instead of 8 p.m. and had one cultural resources manager to watch thousands of petroglyphs and other archaeological sites over 242,000 acres, when she needed five.\par \par \uc2 "How does one person take care of all that resource and protect it?" Fritzke says. She blames short staffing for an increase in vandalism and graffiti at Capitol Reef.\par \par \uc2 Pam Rasmussen says she noticed short staffing during her visit to Yellowstone in July. She joined hundreds of other visitors exploring geothermal features at the park's popular Norris Geyser Basin. Uniformed staff mostly directed traffic on the roads above.\par \par \uc2 "There was no one around to explain what we were seeing. Like, is the mud in the geyser about to explode?" said the 64-year-old from San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 Others in the park said they didn't notice the understaffing.\par \par \uc2 "I felt like most of the places that we stopped there's been staff there and they've been able to give us more information on where to go," said 39-year-old Jose Flores of Riverside, Calif.\par \par \uc2 A Yellowstone spokeswoman said the park was too busy to respond to specific concerns. She pointed to online advice on downloading a self-guiding app.\par \par \uc2 At Carlsbad Caverns National Park in New Mexico, a spokesman says temporary hiring delays have caused some disruptions of tours.\par \par \uc2 Susan Lynch, from El Paso, Texas, says she tried this summer to book one such tour but was told the only staff guide available was booked. When she used to visit years ago, several guides were on hand to lead tours.\par \par \uc2 "It just makes me think we are not being good stewards of this gem we have got," says Lynch, 62.\par \par \uc2 Millions descend on a 6-mile stretch of Zion Canyon every year.\par \par \uc2 Zion increasingly relies on its nonprofit partner, the Zion National Park Forever Project. The organization provides significant funding needed for visitor education, which includes the junior ranger program. Budget shortfalls also affect departments such as search and rescue, says Natalie Britt, president and CEO of Zion Forever.\par \par \uc2 More visitors means more search-and-rescue missions. Zion Forever is trying to raise $58,000 for needs such as new equipment to help stabilize people in cardiac arrest so they can be transported out of the canyon.\par \par \uc2 Zion has a robust volunteer program and employs several interns, says Amanda Rowland, the park's acting spokeswoman. Volunteers work in invasive-species management and serve as campground hosts.\par \par \uc2 The park received federal funding to remodel its popular campground at the south entrance. Meanwhile, long lines to enter the park, board the shuttle and find parking have become commonplace. Crowding puts more pressure on natural resources, emergency services and septic systems.\par \par \uc2 "That gets a little concerning if we forget what normal should look like," says Jack Burns, a recent head of commercial services at Zion. He says Zion can't build its way out of the problem with adding more shuttles or parking lots because volume has exceeded capacity.\par \par \uc2 Officials are considering seasonal park-wide reservations, or a timed-entry system to help manage congestion and effects on the nearby communities while preserving the visitor experience, says Susan McPartland, who leads visitor-use management.\par \par \uc2 Attendance exceeding some parks' capacity creates the need for new strategies to protect natural and cultural resources and improve visitor experiences, a \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Many workers at national parks worry that it's getting harder to give visitors a proper appreciation for the parks and wilderness in general. "Lack of staffing encourages people to run amok and treat it like \uc2 Disney\uc2 , something that can easily be rebuilt," says Zach Alan, 55, who has logged more than 5,000 volunteer hours in numerous Western parks.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240821ek8l0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
