\par\par \b \uc2 Washington Can Give America a Raise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan S. Blinder \par \uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The presidential candidates aren't fixated only on grocery prices. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has also proposed raising the federal minimum wage, which has been stuck at $7.25 an hour since 2009. She's got a strong case for making a change.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with the most compelling argument: In a country as rich as the U.S., no one should work for $7.25 an hour or less. Call it the humanitarian case for a higher minimum wage. The argument rests on value judgments, on which people may disagree. But recent polling shows widespread agreement -- including 89% of Democrats and 84% of Republicans -- that $7.25 an hour is insufficient to support "a decent quality of life."\par \par \uc2 That wording doesn't necessarily imply support for a large increase in the minimum wage. But the same poll found that 76% of Republicans and 91% of Democrats support boosting it to $12 an hour. In our era of hyperpartisanship, that much agreement on anything is stunning.\par \par \uc2 Economists have long believed that the best method for reducing income inequalities is through the tax-transfer system. Let the market dish out rewards, and then modify inequalities deemed to be too large via progressive taxes, transfer payments and the like. Unfortunately, that approach often runs into partisan roadblocks, with Democrats in favor and Republicans opposed.\par \par \uc2 One way out of this conundrum is to rely more on what some economists call "predistribution," that is, reducing market inequalities rather than trying to equalize incomes at a later date. Raising the minimum wage tops the list of predistribution policies by boosting workers' wages on the front end.\par \par \uc2 Such arguments occasion familiar responses: Wouldn't raising the minimum wage kill jobs by pricing some low-wage workers out of the market? Wouldn't it therefore hurt some of those it was intended to help? That's what most economists (including me) used to think -- but research has shown it isn't the case.\par \par \uc2 A fascinating stream of research, kicked off in a seminal 1994 paper by economists \uc2 David Card\uc2  and \uc2 Alan Krueger\uc2 , demonstrated that modest minimum-wage increases result in few or no job losses. When subsequent studies appeared with the same verdict, economists scratched their heads. Doesn't the demand curve for labor slope downward like the demand curve for wheat? If you raise the minimum wage, won't firms hire less labor? It seemed obvious, but the data refuted it. What was going on that simple economic theory missed?\par \par \uc2 Several things, but I'll dwell on only one. For generations, economists have known and written about monopsony -- the counterpart of monopoly, on the buyer's side rather than the seller's. Just as monopoly sellers charge higher prices, monopsony buyers pay lower prices. Just ask \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's suppliers.\par \par \uc2 That potentially includes the price of labor -- the wage. Nevertheless, economists believed monopsony in labor markets was rare. There are too many potential employers. It's hard to name even one "company town" where a single firm is one of the few places to work.\par \par \uc2 That, however, takes the monopsony concept too literally. Just as literal monopolies are rare (iPhones have competitors), so too are literal monopsonies (\uc2 Walmart\uc2  has competitors). When we refer to monopoly power, we mean that firms like \uc2 Apple\uc2  can raise prices without losing too many customers. Many firms have similar monopsony power, meaning they can reduce wages without losing their entire workforce.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  limits monopoly power but has mostly ignored labor-market monopsony on the mistaken view that it isn't there. The minimum wage is its main tool for combating labor-market monopsony, but it has become woefully insufficient.\par \par \uc2 The cost of living, according to the consumer-price index, has risen more than 45% since lawmakers last raised the minimum wage 15 years ago, and average hourly wages have risen about 60%. About two-thirds of state governments now have minimum wages higher than the federal level. Among the highest are the District of Columbia ($17.50), California ($16), and New York ($15 to $16 depending on the region). At the other end of the spectrum, some states -- including Alabama, Mississippi and Texas -- have either adopted or defaulted to the federal minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 So there are blue-red differences -- but, if polls are correct, there's also a strong appetite for change. Raising the federal minimum wage enjoys remarkable bipartisan political support. It's the right thing to do morally and socially, and it would generate more economic good (higher incomes at the bottom) than harm (job losses). What are we waiting for?\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blinder is a professor of economics and public affairs at Princeton. He served as vice chairman of the Federal Reserve, 1994-96.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: Blinder Misrepresents Minimum Wage Studies" -- WSJ Aug. 23, 2024)\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: What the Minimum-Wage Argument Conceals" -- WSJ Aug. 28, 2024)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240821ek8l00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
