\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Possible Rail Strike Leads \uc2 Maersk\uc2  to Cut Some Canada Cargo\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 545 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- Danish shipping line \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  on Monday said it would stop accepting Canada-bound cargo that needs to be moved by rail, the latest move by freight operators to brace for a possible work stoppage this week at Canada's two main railroads.\par \par \uc2 In a notice posted to customers, \uc2 Maersk\uc2  said the carrier has "regrettably decided" to halt acceptance of any Canada-bound shipments that require movement by rail, or where the cargo weight exceeds certain heavy-truck thresholds.\par \par \uc2 The action is the latest move in the freight sector to limit operations ahead of a potential strike or lockout involving more than 9,000 unionized workers at both Canadian National Railway and Canadian Pacific Kansas City that could come as soon as Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Teamsters Canada Rail Conference on Sunday said it has served 72-hour strike notice to CPKC and received a lockout notice from \uc2 Canadian National Railway\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Canadian National said railroad and union negotiators worked over the weekend on a new deal, but "no meaningful progress has occurred, and the parties remain very far apart."\par \par \uc2 CPKC said the economic uncertainty a possible rail stoppage is causing "could be ended today if the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  would agree to binding arbitration."\par \par \uc2 A labor disruption could have major ramifications for North American supply chains and the lackluster Canadian economy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\uc2  estimates that Canada in the first half of this year exported about $38 million in agriculture products by rail.\par \par \uc2 "The economic repercussions of rail work stoppage of this magnitude would be devastating locally, and given the interconnectivity, consequently impact the movement of goods in the United States and beyond," the \uc2 USDA\uc2  said in a note last week.\par \par \uc2 Last year, a roughly two-week strike involving more than 7,000 workers at British Columbia ports disrupted shipping operations across the Pacific Northwest. Authorities estimate that the strike affected shipments totaling 10 billion Canadian dollars, or the equivalent of $7.3 billion, and reduced Canada's economic output by C$1 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  on Sunday said it issued a strike notice to CPKC because the Calgary, Alberta-based railroad planned to strip workers of protections contained in previously negotiated agreements. The union, which represents train conductors, locomotive engineers and rail-yard employees, added that Canadian National was demanding concessions that imperil existing working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Both Canadian railroads moved last week to begin limiting the transport of hazardous goods. Canadian National also told customers it would stop accepting all intermodal traffic -- freight shipped by a combination of truck and rail -- from various origins, including the Ports of New York and New Jersey, the Port of Philadelphia and inland points such as Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 The phased shutdown of the two big rail companies is part of an effort to control traffic ahead of a possible labor disruption.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Labor Minister Steven MacKinnon called on the railroads and the union to "do the hard work necessary to reach agreements at the bargaining table and prevent a work stoppage."\par \par \uc2 MacKinnon last week rejected a request from Canadian National to intervene to send talks to binding arbitration, arguing it was up to the railroads and the union to negotiate in good faith.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240820ek8k0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\