\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's CEO to Work Remotely\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 To woo its new chief executive, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  offered a $10 million cash signing bonus and millions more in stock-based compensation. The coffee giant also didn't insist that he move to the company headquarters in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , the outgoing CEO of Chipotle, instead will be able to live in his home in Southern California and can commute to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's head office on a corporate jet.\par \par \uc2 The arrangement thrusts Niccol into the relatively rare position in the U.S. of a supercommuting CEO, and it shows how determined \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  was to pursue him.\par \par \uc2 Most companies still prefer that their executives maintain a primary residence near a headquarters, corporate advisers say, though there have been some notable cases where a top leader lived and worked elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Victoria's Secret\uc2  made a concession last week when it hired Hillary Super from Rihanna's lingerie brand to take over as its next CEO. Super will be based at the retailer's New York City offices, not at the company's headquarters near Columbus, Ohio. Her employment agreement says Super will be required to frequently travel to Columbus, and her travel costs will be covered by \uc2 Victoria's Secret\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO \uc2 David Calhoun\uc2  started working from home when he took over just before the pandemic in 2020 and never relocated near its headquarters in Arlington, Va., The Wall Street Journal previously reported. He regularly commuted via \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's private-jet fleet from two homes -- one in New Hampshire, the other in South Carolina -- to \uc2 Boeing\uc2  headquarters and other offices.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has said that in a complicated, postpandemic work era, its executives must be on the road frequently as part of their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said that Niccol will have his primary office in its Seattle headquarters, while spending time with employees and customers in its stores, facilities and offices around the world. The company characterized it as a hybrid position. He is expected to have a residence in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 In Niccol's offer letter outlining his employment terms, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  says that after his start date, the company will establish a small remote office in Newport Beach, Calif. It will employ an assistant of Niccol's choosing and will pay to maintain that office.\par \par \uc2 After Niccol took on the top job in 2018 at Chipotle, he decided to relocate the company's headquarters from Denver to Southern California, where he resided, in what he described as an attempt to give the brand a fresh start.\par \par \uc2 Not all employees were offered chances to move. In a podcast released earlier this year, Niccol said explaining that to employees was a low point.\par \par \uc2 While rank-and-file employees might not be able to demand such geographic flexibility, companies often make exceptions for senior-level employees as part of late-stage negotiations to hire someone in a key role, said Raheela Anwar, president and CEO of Group 360 Consulting, a corporate advisory firm.\par \par \uc2 Anwar said she has seen cases where a company initially defines its parameters for a job, including the location, but later adjusts to meet the preferences of its most-desired candidate.\par \par \uc2 She saw Niccol's arrangement at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  as an example of a company's doing what was needed to snag a star executive, even if that meant operating differently than in the past. The ability of CEOs to use a corporate jet also means it is much easier for them to hop up to headquarters or to visit multiple offices in the same day, Anwar said.\par \par \uc2 "Brian is not taking the train from Newport or flying \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 ," she said. "He's being picked up."\par \par \uc2 Sometimes it is the headquarters that relocates. In 2022, billionaire \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2  shifted his hedge fund, \uc2 Citadel\uc2 , from Chicago to Miami -- where he also personally moved.\par \par \uc2 He wrote to employees at the time that he viewed Florida as a better corporate environment; \uc2 Citadel\uc2  officials also said crime was a driving factor.\par \par \uc2 Newell Brands\uc2  moved its headquarters in 2016 from Atlanta to Hoboken, N.J., saying it wanted to tap high-tech talent after a big merger. The move also allowed then-CEO \uc2 Michael Polk\uc2 , who lived in New Jersey, to avoid a long commute.\par \par \uc2 After Polk left in 2019, the company moved its headquarters back to Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Heather Haddon contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240820ek8k0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
