\par\par \b \uc2 GM Is Cutting Over 1,000 Workers From Its Global Software Business\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 374 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  is cutting more than a thousand employees globally in its software and services division, a person familiar with the matter said, a partial reversal of a hiring binge in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The cuts include roughly 600 people who are based around the company's Detroit-area headquarters, the person said Monday. A total number of layoffs couldn't be learned.\par \par \uc2 The reduction comes about two months after GM appointed a pair of former \uc2 Apple\uc2  executives to lead its division focused on the digital aspects of its business, such as connected vehicles, infotainment and advanced driver-assistance systems.\par \par \uc2 GM has hired thousands of salaried workers in software and other tech realms in recent years. Traditional automakers are aiming to develop smarter vehicles that can be updated much like smartphones.\par \par \uc2 "As we build GM's future, we must simplify for speed and excellence, make bold choices, and prioritize the investments that will have the greatest impact," a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 In June, GM promoted two former \uc2 Apple\uc2  executives, Baris Cetinok and Dave Richardson, to run its software and services business. Both men were hired last year under Mike Abbott, another former \uc2 Apple\uc2  executive who stepped down as GM's software chief in March, citing health reasons.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts came after Cetinok and Richardson did a review of the automaker's software division and decided to streamline its operations, the person with knowledge of the cuts said.\par \par \uc2 GM and other legacy automakers have raced in recent years to catch up to competitors such as \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and China-based electric-vehicle startups with software that can add features or improve the vehicle's operation via wireless updates.\par \par \uc2 Car companies are pursuing revenue growth by persuading car owners to sign up for vehicle updates and services, such as hands-free driving and other features that automate some functions in certain situations.\par \par \uc2 GM in 2021 said it was targeting an additional $20 billion to $25 billion in revenue from connected-car services by the end of the decade.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, GM said it would bring on 8,000 tech-focused workers in areas such as electric vehicles, software and autonomous driving.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240820ek8k0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
