\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 On the Clock: Diversity vs. Merit Issue Affects Us All --- After my column, readers provided insightful feedback on the DEI or MEI debate being raised in some business circles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 1,086 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If only you'd all talk to each other as candidly as you write to me -- our workplaces might be better for it.\par \par \uc2 My inbox swelled recently after I chronicled the debate over whether companies should put diversity, equity and inclusion or merit, excellence and intelligence at the center of personnel decisions. Some emails argued that MEI is a racist dog whistle. Others insisted DEI is code for sidelining white men. Between the poles, I received thank-yous for a "willingness to work near the hot wire," as one reader put it.\par \par \uc2 No matter where readers stood, the prevailing sentiment was that companies should hire the best workers they can find without holding people's identities against them.\par \par \uc2 I'm convinced that people on opposite sides of this hot-button issue aren't as far apart as they appear. Mutual suspicion -- particularly in this political climate -- gets in the way of discussion and understanding. Still, there are reasonable people capable of changing their minds or developing more nuanced views.\par \par \uc2 Take the assistant city manager in Texas who wrote to me saying he aspires to be a city manager but has gotten fewer than 10 interviews out of 200-plus job applications in the past six months.\par \par \uc2 He was one of four finalists for a city-manager position in Kansas but said he was rejected before the hiring committee interviewed the last candidate, a woman. The timeline made him wonder whether he had been a real contender or if the process was preordained to end with a woman or person of color getting the nod.\par \par \uc2 Had he followed up to find out who ultimately landed the job, I asked. When he did, he discovered it was a fellow white man who was clearly the most qualified candidate.\par \par \uc2 "Proof that sometimes when you think the narrative is one thing, it might not be so," he said.\par \par \uc2 He would never say any of this publicly because he fears criticizing, or even questioning, DEI efforts would hurt his career prospects.\par \par \uc2 Remaining silent is understandable but regrettable, says Fayruz Kirtzman, a senior client partner at \uc2 Korn Ferry\uc2  who advises companies on DEI. She wrote to me, lamenting what she considers a false choice between merit and diversity.\par \par \uc2 The way she sees it, DEI done right is about giving everyone a fair shot to show their excellence, not elevating underqualified candidates to hit demographic targets.\par \par \uc2 Reality sometimes falls short of that ideal, but it's hard to improve hiring and promotions if people stew in their frustrations instead of sharing them constructively, she told me on a Zoom call.\par \par \uc2 "People really want to have a dialogue, and I encourage anyone to ask questions in a respectful manner," Kirtzman said.\par \par \uc2 A lot of DEI critics tell me they've sat through staff meetings where human-resources leaders assert that diversity is good for the bottom line, even though a study that helped form the basis of that belief has come under new scrutiny. And they're weary of the refrain that a rising tide lifts all boats.\par \par \uc2 That may be true in the big picture, they say. But when a job or promotion is on the line, only one person wins.\par \par \uc2 Bad personal experiences sometimes lead to sweeping conclusions.\par \par \uc2 Francis Driscoll, a 29-year-old recruiter turned oyster farmer, suggested in a \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  message that the goal of DEI efforts was to shove white men like him out of the workforce.\par \par \uc2 In a follow-up phone call, he explained he had been told by a manager in a previous job that the company needed to hire "fewer Brads and Chads."\par \par \uc2 He had understood that to mean fewer white men.\par \par \uc2 "I genuinely don't think it's coming from a place of contempt for white people or anything like that," he said. "I think it's coming from a place of wanting to make up for past wrongs, but sometimes it leads to discrimination the other way."\par \par \uc2 Quilen Blackwell, president of Southside Blooms, a Chicago florist, told me DEI initiatives help him win contracts for fancy events at venues like the Ritz-Carlton and Field Museum of Natural History. His organization is a nonprofit that hires mostly people of color, generally 16- to 25-year-olds at risk of gang violence.\par \par \uc2 Some clients are eager to give his group opportunities. Others doubt his employees' skills. He encourages his 30 team members to quiet skeptics with excellent work and consider that customers' qualms might be rooted in the young workers' inexperience, rather than racism.\par \par \uc2 "When you just assume someone's racist, it shuts everything down and is a missed opportunity for both you and that person to learn and grow," said Blackwell, 39.\par \par \uc2 Labels and acronyms can be useful shorthands, but simplicity invites multiple interpretations. A vague phrase like "gun control" generates strong, opposing reactions while a specific policy proposal like universal background checks produces more than 90% agreement, for example.\par \par \uc2 DEI and MEI likewise mean different things to different people, said Alexander Heffner, host of "The Open Mind" on \uc2 PBS\uc2  and "Breaking Bread With Alexander" on Bloomberg TV.\par \par \uc2 "The perception that merit or equity is exclusionary is mind-boggling, but misinterpreting each other's language is a huge part of the problem," he told me. "Maybe we need a new lexicon."\par \par \uc2 Or a new willingness to seek the intent behind people's word choices.\par \par \uc2 Several readers wrote to me because my column included human-resources consultant Seena Hodges, who calls herself the Woke Coach.\par \par \uc2 They assumed she must have adopted the moniker as a deliberate provocation.\par \par \uc2 Hodges started calling herself the Woke Coach in 2018, before the term was so polarizing. She picked the name partly as a reference to the Wide Awakes, a group of Republicans who worked to elect \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  president in 1860. Her use of woke was meant to be more of an olive branch than a jab. Yet it's easy to misunderstand the gesture if you get hung up on the word.\par \par \uc2 "To be woke, for me, means each of us is the sum of our own lived experiences, and we have to awaken to the experiences of other people," Hodges said.\par \par \uc2 That's a sentiment many of us can likely agree on if we take time to talk and listen to each other.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240819ek8j0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
