\par\par \b \uc2 Deere\uc2  Slashes Jobs, Slows Production To Navigate Farm-Economy Downturn\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 903 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Deere & Co.\uc2 , the world's largest tractor manufacturer, is racing to shrink as it contends with a slowing farm economy.\par \par \uc2 Demand for \uc2 Deere\uc2 's tractors, crop harvesters and other farming equipment has plunged after years of generating record profits from robust sales. As a result, the company has slowed activity at assembly plants, laid off more than 2,000 production workers and trimmed hundreds of salaried employees.\par \par \uc2 Deere\uc2  has reacted to past downturns by trying to wring revenue out of a softening market with sales incentives or equipment leases. But this time around, the company is aggressively shedding expenses and bracing for a hard landing in a U.S. farm economy that had been buoyed in recent years by high prices for farm commodities and federal payments to farmers.\par \par \uc2 "We are acting more quickly," Chief Executive \uc2 John May\uc2  said during a Thursday conference call with analysts. "It's all about taking out excess costs and restructuring the business. In the past, that might have taken us two years to two and a half years to react."\par \par \uc2 Deere\uc2  reported that its profit fell 42% in the latest quarter from the prior year to $1.7 billion. Overall equipment sales decreased by 20%. The results were better than analysts expected and \uc2 Deere\uc2  maintained its outlook on full-year profit, helping send the company's shares up more than 7% in regular trading.\par \par \uc2 The company has shed about 15% of its hourly workforce since November. \uc2 Deere\uc2 's layoffs of salaried workers are a departure from previous downturns, when it had typically relied on voluntary resignations to reduce head count.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Deere\uc2  employees said the company sacrificed loyal employees for short-term cost reductions by aggressively cutting payrolls. \uc2 Deere\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Deere\uc2 's global workforce grew 24% from 2013 to 2023, but its employment growth in the U.S. and Canada was roughly flat. It reported about 34,000 employees in the U.S. and Canada as of Oct. 31.\par \par \uc2 The company bulked up staffing starting around 2021 to expedite orders when shortages of parts and materials after the pandemic created long wait times for equipment. \uc2 Deere\uc2  employees said two or three factory workers were assigned to some jobs that had been handled by a single worker before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Job cuts haven't been the only thing rattling some employees. The company has said it plans to move production of some models of compact construction machinery from Iowa to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Deere\uc2  said the move isn't related to weakening market conditions. The company spent $2.5 billion on its U.S. plants over the past 4 1/2 years and considers the U.S. its primary manufacturing location, a \uc2 Deere\uc2  executive said in a recent interview with Farm Journal. Some employees interpreted the decision as a sign of the company's waning commitment to building machinery in America.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown in production comes amid expectations that falling prices for corn, soybeans and other farm commodities will reduce demand for its equipment. The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  forecasts farm income will decline by about 25% this year from 2023. Higher interest rates are further diminishing farmers' purchasing power.\par \par \uc2 Deere\uc2  accounts for about two-thirds of the high-horsepower tractors sold annually in the U.S. and Canada. Higher equipment demand in the aftermath of the pandemic fueled a sharp increase in \uc2 Deere\uc2 's annual profits from 2019 to 2023 with revenue increasing by 56% during that time.\par \par \uc2 Putting less new equipment into the market helps \uc2 Deere\uc2 's dealers whittle elevated supplies of used gear. \uc2 Deere\uc2  attempted to spur demand during the last market slump by offering farmers short-term leases on new equipment, which ultimately exacerbated a glut in used equipment when farmers returned the machines to dealers after the leases expired.\par \par \uc2 Selling the used tractors and combines now parked on dealers' lots is likely to be more challenging than in the past, dealers and industry analysts said. Newer machines are often more powerful and more expensive than in the past. \uc2 Deere\uc2  and other equipment makers raised new machinery prices repeatedly in recent years to cover higher production costs caused by inflation and supply-chain bottlenecks.\par \par \uc2 Farmers who typically bought used high-horsepower tractors and combines during a downturn for $100,000 to $200,000 are likely to find those models now priced at $300,000 to $450,000, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "We've got a lot of bigger machinery that costs a lot more," said Mitch Helman, sales manager for Sandhills Global, a Nebraska data service for used equipment markets.\par \par \uc2 An extended slump in equipment sales is likely to test \uc2 Deere\uc2 's strategy of offering software subscriptions to improve the performance of older equipment. \uc2 Deere\uc2  has been rolling out technology for self-driving tractors that can plow fields without a driver and sprayers that can distinguish weeds from crops when applying herbicide. CEO May has said he wants the company to generate 10% of its annual revenue from software fees by the end of the decade.\par \par \uc2 One hope for increasing sales: a bumper corn crop and record soybean harvest in the Midwest this year. Dealers said a big harvest will likely convince some farmers to invest in equipment even if the harvest holds down commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 "More bushels will help sell equipment," said Tom Sloan, chairman of Illinois-based \uc2 Sloan Implement\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240816ek8g0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
