\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Trump, Musk and Union Crocodile Tears\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain seems to think free speech ends where union interests begin. The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' chief picked a legal fight with Donald Trump and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  this week, claiming they violated worker protections Monday in a live interview between the former President and the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO.\par \par \uc2 The two-hour conversation spanned topics from radioactive fallout in Japan to the speed of prescription-drug approvals. Mr. Fain was listening closely. He seized on a single line about halfway through. "You're the greatest cutter," Mr. Trump said, praising Mr. Musk's management. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  workers aren't unionized, despite years of \uc2 UAW\uc2  attempts. "They go on strike," Mr. Trump added, "and you say 'That's OK -- you're all gone. You're all gone. So every one of you is gone.'"\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump didn't mention \uc2 Tesla\uc2  by name, and Mr. Musk gave no response beyond a chuckle. Yet the exchange was too much for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . It filed a complaint against both men with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . "Workers cannot be fired for going on strike, and threatening to do so is illegal," the union said.\par \par \uc2 The complaint is a mighty stretch from so little material. Federal labor law bars employers from promising to fire specific workers who mount a legal strike. It doesn't prohibit a politician who isn't the actual employer from musing about it to a CEO in hypothetical terms.\par \par \uc2 But the complaint is typical of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , which seems to find political PR an easier job than persuading workers. Instead of conceding after Mercedes employees declined to join in May, the union demanded another election, accusing the car maker of "egregious illegal behavior." What was the offense? One gripe was that Mercedes held meetings to dissuade employees from joining up. In other words, free speech.\par \par \uc2 Yet unions are more than happy to welcome political bias to their picket lines. President Biden endorsed a major auto strike last year and flew to Michigan to rally with workers, breaking a century-old norm of neutrality from the Oval Office. That show of government force raised the pressure on the auto makers to cut a deal, yet somehow the \uc2 UAW\uc2  didn't notice any intimidation. The same goes for pro-union remarks from Sen. Bernie Sanders, J.D. Vance, and others in the Big Labor caucus.\par \par \uc2 Accusing an opponent of playing unfairly is often the first resort of a losing team. In open elections, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has struggled to win workers to its side, and one way Mr. Fain hopes to change that is by lobbying government to coerce businesses and workers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240815ek8f0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
