\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Ex-CEO Criticizes \uc2 Google\uc2 , Retracts It --- Schmidt says he misspoke in faulting company's approach to work-life balance\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph De Avila \par \uc2 453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Eric Schmidt\uc2 , ex-CEO and executive chairman at \uc2 Google\uc2 , walked back remarks in which he said his former company was losing the artificial intelligence race because of its remote-work policies.\par \par \uc2 "I misspoke about \uc2 Google\uc2  and their work hours," Schmidt said in an email Wednesday to The Wall Street Journal. "I regret my error."\par \par \uc2 Schmidt, who left \uc2 Google\uc2  parent \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's board more than five years ago, spoke earlier at a wide-ranging discussion at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  in California. He criticized \uc2 Google\uc2 's remote-work policies in response to a question about \uc2 Google\uc2  competing with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Video of the talk was posted this week by Stanford Online, a division of the university which offers online courses. The video, which had more than 40,000 views as of Wednesday afternoon, has since been set to private.\par \par \uc2 Schmidt said he asked for the video to be taken down. He declined to comment further. Stanford didn't respond to a request for comment about the video.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Google\uc2  decided that work-life balance and going home early and working from home was more important than winning," Schmidt said at the talk. "The reason startups work is because the people work like hell."\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  have instituted similar return-to-office policies since the pandemic. Both companies have mandated that people come to the office three days per week since 2022.\par \par \uc2 The search-engine company on Wednesday touted the benefits of hybrid work. The company said it reaches out to employees who don't show up three days a week to remind them about the in-person requirements.\par \par \uc2 Schmidt joins a long list of corporate leaders, including \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who have complained about work-from-home policies, saying they make companies less efficient and less competitive. Dimon said in an annual letter a few years ago that people in the upper ranks "cannot lead from behind a desk or in front of a screen." Musk has said workers need "a minimum of 40 hours in the office per week."\par \par \uc2 "Flexible work arrangements don't slow down our work," Alphabet Workers Union, which represents more than 1,000 employees in the U.S. and Canada, said in a post on X. "Understaffing, shifting priorities, constant layoffs, stagnant wages and lack of follow-through from management on projects -- these factors slow \uc2 Google\uc2  workers down every day."\par \par \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  had roughly 182,000 employees as of the end of last year, according to its annual report.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has been playing defense on AI since \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  launched ChatGPT in late 2022.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240815ek8f0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
