\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: \uc2 UAW\uc2  Alleges Trump and Musk Sought To Intimidate Workers in X Discussion\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph De Avila and Christopher Otts \par \uc2 535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  filed federal labor charges against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , alleging they attempted to intimidate workers during their conversation on Musk's social-media platform.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 , one of the nation's largest unions, seized on remarks Trump made Monday in a conversation with Musk, in which the Republican presidential nominee commented on how an employer should handle striking workers.\par \par \uc2 "I mean, I look at what you do," Trump said during the talk with Musk. "You walk in, you say, 'You want to quit?' They go on strike, I won't mention the name of the company, but they go on strike and you say, 'That's OK, you're all gone. You're all gone. So, every one of you is gone.'"\par \par \uc2 The charges that were filed Tuesday by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  allege both men threatened workers who participate in protected labor activity, including walkouts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  highlighted Trump's comment in a press release describing the charges but didn't reference any statements made by Musk during the interview.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for Musk and the Trump campaign didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Under federal law, employees conducting lawful strikes for economic reasons, such as higher wages, can't be discharged by their employers. They can be replaced under certain circumstances, according to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 , which has endorsed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the presidential nominee for the Democrats, has been eager to blunt the former president's appeal to working-class voters by painting him as a friend of the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 "When we say Trump stands against everything our union stands for, this is what we mean," said \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  filed charges with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  against Musk as a representative of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , an electric-vehicle maker at which the union has been trying to organize workers for years.\par \par \uc2 Musk has previously disagreed with the idea of unions, and another of his companies, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , has challenged the constitutionality of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 . Speaking at a conference last year, Musk said unions "try to create negativity in a company, and create a sort of lords and peasants situation."\par \par \uc2 "If \uc2 Tesla\uc2  gets unionized, it will be because we deserved it and we failed in some way," Musk said. "But we certainly try hard to ensure the prosperity of everyone."\par \par \uc2 The union's charges against Trump and Musk are "amorphous" and it isn't clear what kind of practical impact they might have on the two men, said Michael Duff, a former \uc2 NLRB\uc2  investigator who teaches labor law at \uc2 St. Louis University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Its principal value is political," Duff said. The union would have to show Trump's statement could be interpreted as an employer threatening a specific group of workers, he added.\par \par \uc2 Musk, as CEO of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , would be more easily considered an employer if the comment relates to auto workers, Duff said. The problem with the charge against Musk is the notion that the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  chief could have liability for Trump's words rather than his own, he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240814ek8e0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
