\par\par \b \uc2 Airbus\uc2  Fails to Capitalize on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Chaos\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Katz \par \uc2 1,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Christian Scherer\uc2  took the job of running \uc2 Airbus\uc2 's commercial aircraft division at the start of the year, the gig looked like a slam dunk.\par \par \uc2 The plane maker had just smashed its record for annual orders, airlines were still clamoring for more jets and production was ramping up. The company's only significant rival, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , had been flung into a fresh and escalating crisis after a door-size panel blew off the side of a 737 midflight.\par \par \uc2 Since then, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  has been dogged by delays, prompting the company to cut its annual delivery guidance and defer a long-heralded production target. First-half orders were less than one-third of the year-earlier intake, and the company's stock is now down more than 20% from its record high in March.\par \par \uc2 It is a frustrating change in fortunes for the world's biggest jet manufacturer, which was confident it could capitalize this year on a postpandemic surge in demand. Instead, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  is mired in supply-chain issues.\par \par \uc2 "I thought we were going to be in a better place," Scherer, a company lifer who for years has been focused on ramping up production, said in an interview. "There's the whole future to prepare, but now there's also the present to manage much more than we thought."\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  said late last month that it had been blindsided by some of the new delays, which span engines, landing gear, seats and toilets, all of which were holding back production. These components are meant to arrive just in time for the company to install on newly built jets, limiting its ability to react to last-minute snags.\par \par \uc2 Separately, last Friday, a 14-year-old plane made by \uc2 Airbus\uc2 's joint venture with Italy's Leonardo fell from the sky in Brazil, killing all 62 on board. Officials are investigating the cause of the crash. Aviation experts have pointed to the possibility of ice buildup on the plane's wings.\par \par \uc2 The company's challenges come as \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is working to get past its struggles with the start of \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , a new chief executive. Ortberg is well versed in the aerospace supply chain, having spent much of his career running the predecessor to RTX, one of the biggest suppliers to both \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 Airbus\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Boeing\uc2  will never be a weaker competitor than it was at the beginning of this year," said Sash Tusa, an analyst at Agency Partners.\par \par \uc2 The next few years could be critical to how the balance of power between \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 Airbus\uc2  might shift as the industry looks toward a new generation of aircraft programs, which typically cost billions of dollars to develop. \uc2 Airbus\uc2 , which is sitting on close to $9 billion in cash, has a big head start, with \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's total debt reaching nearly $58 billion in its latest update.\par \par \uc2 Much of the aviation industry is being hindered by delays, with airlines complaining of monthslong waits for aircraft from \uc2 Airbus\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . The plane makers and their suppliers have been contending with the loss of swaths of seasoned workers who left during the pandemic and whom they have had to replace with new, inexperienced recruits.\par \par \uc2 "You need to train those people, you need to retrain them, you need to double-check their work. It's just extra effort," Scherer said. At \uc2 Airbus\uc2 , the level of seniority required for an employee to sign off on work has been increased to mitigate risk.\par \par \uc2 The European plane maker has launched an internal program called Project Lead to focus teams on the supply-chain challenges and cut out some of its own smaller side projects that might distract from the core goal of boosting production.\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  also has increased the number of staffers it has working at its biggest suppliers' manufacturing sites to help navigate bottlenecks and track progress. The suppliers include Safran of France, \uc2 Honeywell\uc2 , \uc2 Pratt & Whitney\uc2  and \uc2 CFM International\uc2 , a joint venture between \uc2 Safran\uc2  and \uc2 GE Aerospace\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the so-called joint improvement plan teams don't necessarily mean \uc2 Airbus\uc2  will get the parts it needs on time, the company says it is helping to improve transparency and increase cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Scherer said he doesn't know how long it will take for the industry to get over these latest delays. \uc2 Airbus\uc2  has indicated it might not get all the parts the company needs to meet end-of-year targets.\par \par \uc2 "This is an issue that will be prolonged -- three years, four years, five years, maybe 10 years, I don't know," said \uc2 Jozsef Varadi\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 Wizz Air\uc2 , an all-\uc2 Airbus\uc2  operator that had aimed to double its fleet to 500 aircraft by 2030. It now expects to reach that milestone in 2032.\par \par \uc2 Varadi said he expects to receive compensation from \uc2 Airbus\uc2  for each delayed aircraft.\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2 's supply-chain struggles are frustrating for Scherer, who joined the company in 1984 and previously led its sales team as chief commercial officer for five years.\par \par \uc2 Scherer had spent much of the pandemic trying to persuade \uc2 Airbus\uc2 's biggest suppliers that they needed to be ready for an inevitable scramble for aircraft when passengers returned to the skies. They didn't heed his warning, he said.\par \par \uc2 "I'm disappointed," Scherer said. "We've been preaching out there, saying 'Guys, here is the curve.' That some of the large, sophisticated players who have their hands on the pulse of this industry would question that is disappointing."\par \par \uc2 As Covid-19 decimated air travel, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  pushed airlines to take aircraft they didn't want so the jet maker could keep production ticking along at a rate high enough to allow it to quickly bounce back once demand recovered.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  announced plans to return production of the A320neo, its bestselling rival to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's 737 MAX, to prepandemic levels in 2023, and move to a rate of 75 a month by 2025.\par \par \uc2 That production rate would be the highest ever for a commercial airliner and help supercharge the company's market share lead over its U.S. rival.\par \par \uc2 In January of this year, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  said its backlog had surged to a record 8,598 aircraft in 2023 and that its A320 was sold out until early into the next decade. The company has said it turned some customers away because of the lack of availability.\par \par \uc2 Before this year, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  had struggled to work through orders as rapidly as it wanted, partly because of its own manufacturing challenges. At the start of 2024, the company was confident its production was on a firmer footing -- only then to be sidetracked by the recent supply issues.\par \par \uc2 In June, \uc2 Airbus\uc2  deferred its 75-a-month target for a second time. The company now expects to reach that rate in 2027. It also cut back its goal for this year from about 800 aircraft deliveries to 770.\par \par \uc2 That number compares with 735 delivered in 2023.\par \par \uc2 "Our trajectory is ambitious," Scherer said. "We're on it, but our supply chain is not. We've got to whip the supply chain into shape."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw536\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8040\pichgoal7200
